In article <3367F108.4D78@nortel.ca>, Ian Willmott  <willmott@nortel.ca> wrote:

>When both banks are populated, the LEDs progress through the
>sequence listed in the manual, ending with 1010-0001, correctly
>indicating 256K cache. I can't think of any explanation except that
>one of the SIMMs is defective.

And you've tried each combination of a pair of SIMMs in each of
the two banks? If your system only works with all four banks loaded,
that strikes me as a possible motherboard problem; of the two
motherboards I've tested, both work with only one pair of 16 MB 60
ns parity SIMMs. (The other option is that there might be a problem
with the refresh timing on the SIMMs, I suppose, but that's out of
my depth.)

>Next problem: nothing happens after this. I have been given to
>understand that these boards are shipped with the ARC console in
>flash, and that this will use either the video card or COM1. Nothing
>on either one - I put an oscilloscope on the COM1 Tx line and it's
>stuck at -12V.

Are you sure you've got the real TX line? If you bought the
motherboard from the same vendor I bought mine from, you got an
early revision of the OEM manual with incorrect pinouts for the
serial port. The `standard' connectors I got from the computer shop
down the street were not the same as the `standard' connectors that
other people have had work for them, either. Grab a copy of the
latest OEM guide from ftp.digital.com (under /pub/DEC/Alpha somewhere,
I believe) and use that as a reference to build a serial connector.


>Question: the manual claims that the ARC and SRM consoles are
>set for NO STOP BITS. Is this correct?

No. I use 9600,N,8,1.

>I don't understand why milo doesn't use the video card; I thought that
>the built-in x86 emulator meant that any VGA card would work.

As far as the video card goes, the firmware will emulate the 8086
instructions in the ROM on the card to initialise it. It could be
that this doesn't work well with the ROM on the ATI card or something.
It would be worth trying another card. For me it worked for both
a cheap S3 PCI and a cheap Trident ISA card.

>Note: I previously posted a source for these cache chips at $1.33CDN
>each ($9.73CDN for the 128Kx8 part). As far as I can tell at this
>point, they do in fact work.

I bought the 128K part, and they are working fine in my AXPpci33
right now.

cjs
