Thomas Gunnar Sparks wrote:
> no, but linux has 0 reliability or real support team... Who'd sell a
> product they didn't get support for? who'd sell a product that they
> didn't believe in? I wouldnt...

This is misleading.  Linux is very reliable, much more so than many
commercial operating systems.  It's certainly more reliable than Windows
NT or anything else from Micro$oft.  I've *never* had Linux crash.  The
GNU utilities included are also more stable than the typical utilities
included by a Unix vendor.  I've had Ultrix and Solaris utilities crash
when I tried to do something twisted, while those in Linux never
crashed.  
As for support, there are all these HOWTO files and then there are the
comp.os.linux.* newsgroups.  It's true that this may not be enough to
satisfy some people.  I think that there are companies that you can pay
for support in such cases.

> OpenBSD/Alpha works great and is a fairly solid product. However its
> still in testing, I doubt much will be released till its stable
> (unlike linux where a new system is tossed out every day or so)

Sure, OpenBSD is nice.  However, I don't see why it would be any better
than Linux.  The fact that kernel updates come so quickly just means
that there are very many people working on it.  Sure, there may be bugs
there, but I have no reason to beleive that VMS, Digital Unix, OpenBSD
or Windows NT don't have bugs.  The fact that Linux kernel changes are
released so often is a good thing.  For example, after the ping of death
vulnerability became known, there was a fix for Linux really fast.

> For some reason it doesn't support the floppy drives, but it
> supports more hardware than linux...

Linux seems to have more PC hardware support than OpenBSD.  It's true
that OpenBSD runs on more platforms, but that may not matter too much if
you just want to put Unix on an Alpha system.  

Summary:  TRY it before saying it sucks!

