> The "story" I heard from within Apple is that they considered all the
> architectures and deals were discussed with all the players before going
> with PowerPC. PowerPC was third or fourth on the list from a technical
[...]

I'm pretty sure the 64-bit version of the MOS tech 6502
family was the top choice.  Dubbed the 650264, the chip
was to power a "Machotosh" that would blend the highly
successful Apple IIGS and the Mac into one killer platform,
which would run the large installed base of 8-bit Apple
apps at stunning speed.  An emulator was available to
run Tandy CoCo software in stunning 24-bit color.  The
CoCo display was still limited to 160 by 100 pixels, but
up to 64 instances of the CoCo programs could be run on the
screen at once, for the first time utilizing the MPI
features built into Turtle LOGO90.



