On 28 Apr 1997 22:15:11 -0700, Curt Sampson <cjs@cynic.portal.ca> wrote:
>In article <5k35s3$pv4@aurora.cs.athabascau.ca>,
>Ross Alexander <rwa@cs.athabascau.ca> wrote:
>>cjs@cynic.portal.ca (Curt Sampson) writes:
>>
>>Or if you've got filesystems on partitions larger than 2^32.
>
>Plenty of 32-bit operating systems deal with that just fine. There's
>no reason the maximum size of a filesystem has to be limited to
>the maximum size of an integer on a machine.

	Actually, due to the architecture of Unix and the lseek() call,
	the size of a volume should not exceed that of the maximum positive
	integer value of a long (i.e. (2**31)-1, 0x7ffffff).  Some folks
	(IBM AIX 4.x) got around this by using unsigned longs for the
	filesystem seeks (since there isn't that much of a reason to
	support a whence of 1 or 2) but *FILES* were still limited to
	the 2GB size.  This is tacked to the "long" size and is a major
	reason why all kinds of neat tricks are used to exceed the 2GB
	F/S limit.

	Solaris is still a 32bit OS despite running on the UltraSparcs
	which are capable of supporting a 64bit OS natively.  The DEC
	Alpha systems (both -Server and -Station) running DUX (OSF/1,
	DEC Unix, etc) are native 64bit OSes so these can handle *awesome*
	filesystems;  Some of the RAID sets are PFB (Pretty Big).

	*I* like the DEC Alpha enough that my next upgrade *ain't* gonna
	be a Pentium or it's deviants;  I'd like to avoid the 8080
	instruction set for a change.

