In article <5k74ma$44h@darkstar.ucsc.edu>,
Thomas Gunnar Sparks <tgs@cats.ucsc.edu> wrote:

>OpenBSD/Alpha works great and is a fairly solid product. . . .
>For some reason it doesn't support the floppy drives, but it
>supports more hardware than linux...

OpenBSD/Alpha is really just NetBSD/Alpha, as far as I'm aware. I
don't believe any significant active work is being done on the
OpenBSD Alpha port, they just import the NetBSD code every once in
a while and check to see if it boots. Of course, you still get the
OpenBSD MI kernel and userland, if you prefer those.

It's currently possible to boot a NetBSD kernel from floppy, but
not to use the floppy once the OS is running. This is because we're
still putting together a proper MI DMA structure, among other
things. Once that's in place, which should be sometime later this
year, pretty much any i386 drivers should work on the Alpha as
well. This means not only the floppy and IDE drivers, but also
support for things like putting an Adaptec 1542 in to an ISA slot
on an alpha and having it Just Work (doing appropriate bounce
buffering and everything).

There probably will be some alignment stuff in many drivers that
needs to be fixed as well; the Alpha is a rather more fussy about
alignment than the i386 is, as I found when I got the 3Com Etherlink
III drivers working on it. But that stuff isn't hard to fix.

