>> >  The practical upshot of all this is, a good Alpha will run quite a bit
>> >faster then an Intel, even at the same clock speeds.
>> 
>> This is not true. For ballpark figures for integer performance,
>> you can compare a 166 MHz 21064 to a 90 MHz or 100 MHz Pentium,
>> and a 300 MHz 21164 to a 200 MHz Pentium pro.
>
>That depends on a lot of things, including the application and the
>compiler used for the executable.

Indeed, that is why I said `ballpark.' There is, however, no Alpha
compiler in the world for an that will make a 166 MHz 21064 have
integer performance anywhere near a P166 using any reasonable
compiler.

I have no idea why so many people here have this obsession with
`at the same clock speed,' anyway. The Alpha is a RISC architecture
that does less per clock cycle than the CISC-based Pentium
architecture. Part of the whole point of the Alpha is that it can
overcome this by having a lot more clock cycles than a Pentium:
there's a very good reason why Digital is shipping 600 MHz Alpha
CPUs when Intel is barely even testing 300 MHz Pentium-type chips.

