I am having some problems getting my AXPpci33 board to boot. I wonder
if anyone has experienced similar problems or has any suggestions. At
this point just getting a boot loader prompt would be significant
progress.

configuration (all brand new):

166MHz 21066 AXPpci33
4 x 16Mbyte 60ns DRAM parity SIMMs
Alliance AS7C256-15PC cache SRAM (32Kx8 15ns)
ATI 3D Xpression+ 4Mbyte PCI video card (Rage II chipset)

I fabricated the LED diagnostic circuit described in the manual. First
problem: when I power up the machine with only one of the two memory
banks populated (two SIMMs), nothing happens. The LEDs stay stuck at
1111-1110, which the manual describes as "size memory - empty
bank". Nothing on the monitor or COM1. This happens regardless of
which bank is filled; in any case, the manual claims this does not
matter. When both banks are populated, the LEDs progress through the
sequence listed in the manual, ending with 1010-0001, correctly
indicating 256K cache. I can't think of any explanation except that
one of the SIMMs is defective.

Next problem: nothing happens after this. I have been given to
understand that these boards are shipped with the ARC console in
flash, and that this will use either the video card or COM1. Nothing
on either one - I put an oscilloscope on the COM1 Tx line and it's
stuck at -12V. Can't think what the problem is here. This board was
probably manufactured several years ago - does flash ROM lose its
charge if it's not powered up in that long?

Next, I set J28/J29 to boot from a floppy. Put milo.dd (v2.0.29) in the
drive and reset. Different LED sequence, drive accessed. Monitor goes
through several shades of blue and grey, but nothing useful appears.
Oscilloscope shows output on COM1, but my 8088 PClone terminal
emulator (9600,n,8,1) only beeps a few times, nothing intelligible
appears. Question: the manual claims that the ARC and SRM consoles are
set for NO STOP BITS. Is this correct? I never heard of such a thing
before. Most UARTs do not support this. Is milo the same?

I don't understand why milo doesn't use the video card; I thought that
the built-in x86 emulator meant that any VGA card would work.

Any thoughts or experiences on these issues would be appreciated.

Note: I previously posted a source for these cache chips at $1.33CDN
each ($9.73CDN for the 128Kx8 part). As far as I can tell at this
point, they do in fact work. See
http://www.alsc.com/products/prodsram.shtml , check the distributor
list at the end of the datasheets.


