In fact even if you cannot put over 4GB on a box, a 64 bit virtual
address allows things 32 bits will not.

Consider for one example that in a 64 bit space you can memory map
a database of over 4GB and have plenty of address space for other
useful things (like your programs). Even if you wind up "only" keeping
a couple gigs in physical memory due to your access patterns, that
can give major speedups in access. If you don't have the virtual
address space, you get to play this game only by hand with major
distortion of your application, if you can play it at all. (Unix
32 bit filesystems tend to have problems with lseek out beyond 2GB
file sizes, for example.) What "can be done with infinite screwing
around" in terms of compensating for too-small address spaces for your
problem is great. Those of us who learned to make humongous overlay
trees on pdp11 with half a dozen cotrees and hundreds of leaves per
tree and other mapping and hacking techniques can recall them ad nauseam.

However in terms of getting practical work done, having wide enough
address space for your problem is one of the really important
things to ensure. There really IS a difference that 64 bits brings
to the table, even with physical memory small enough to fit into a
32 bit address.



