What a load of rubbish. Just because your operating system supports 64 bit
address spaces doesn't mean you need over 4 gig of RAM to use it. Your
application might like to memory map very large files. Sure it will page when
you reference but you could do it and you would use the efficient paging I/O.
What about wanting a vary large but possibly sparse address space?

Take VMS for instance. The alpha running V7.0 of VMS or higher supports a 64
bit address space. To be compatible with the older versions of VMS the
operating system is still mapped into a 32 bit chunk starting at 0x80000000.
The users low address space is split into 2 0-0x3fffffff (program + heap) and
0x40000000-0x7fffffff (stack and control data). So in VMS you can't have a
chunk of virtual memory bigger than 1GB without using the 64 bit address space.

Your confusing virtual and physical memory.
