On Mon, 28 Apr 1997 15:07:16 GMT, Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop) wrote:
>
>1. If you can't put more than 4 GB RAM on the box, having a 64-bit OS
>   buys you nothing (on the contrary, it's wasting memory).
>

Pick up an introductory text on operating systems and read about
virtual memory.  There are a vast number of problems that need to
cleanly deal with memory spaces larger than 4GB, but don't nessesarily
have to have everything live in physical memory at all times; large
databases and Lisp environments jump to mind immediately.

>
>2. What is preventing you from installing Linux on that machine?
>

While as much of a Linux bigot as any nerd can be, there are any
number of reasons not to go with Linux.  For example, the Digital Unix
compilers generate much better code currently than GCC, et. al.   This
is a big deal for the number crunchers, especially the Fortran types
(BTW, Digital F90 is awfully nice, if that sort of thing is your
particular kink).

A single tool is never right for all jobs...

- Ken




