Question:


Why does Lantronix recommend TCP/IP printing from NT and Win95 instead
of NetBIOS?


Answer:

When printing from Windows NT and Win 95 using Lan Manager, if the
printer flow controls for more than 45 seconds, the NT machine will
display an alert box saying the printer is having problems.  At that
point, it presents the options to restart or cancel the job.
Unfortunately, whatever you select, the NT or Win 95 machine will never
send any more data out the network.

Unfortunately, when this happens, the print server still thinks there
is a valid job in the queue.  It will not time out the job so you need
to type

LOGOUT PORT n

at the local> prompt to get things going again.

NT has the option to increase this timeout and their documentation
suggests upping it for slow devices.  Unfortunately, this does not seem
to work.  You can change the number to whatever you want but after 45
seconds it still times out the job.



================================================================================
Windows NT, Printing Methods


Question:


What is the procedure for installing an print queue to a Lantronix print
server under Windows NT 3.5x?


Answer:


There are 3 methods of printing which are supported by Lantronix print
servers:

1. LPR Configuration:
---------------------

The following installation assumes that TCP/IP protocol and printing
support has been installed in Windows NT server machine. In addition, the
print server must have an IP address assigned via the ARP command.

To use the ARP command, select the Command Prompt icon in the Main group
and issue the following at the prompt:

ARP -S XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX 00-80-A3-XX-XX-XX

XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX  = IP address to be assigned to print server
00-80-A3-XX-XX-XX  = Print server's Ethernet address



To install an lpr print queue:

Run print manager.

In the Printer menu, choose "Create Printer"

Provide a printer name and select your particular printer.

Select Print To:
Select other
Select lpr port
Provide input to the following:
        Name or Address of host providing lpd: xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx
        Name of printer on that machine:       eps_xxxxxx_p1


xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx = IP address of the Lantronix print server
eps_xxxxxx_p1 = name of print service on the print server


2. DLC Configuration -------------------- To print with DLC protocol, a
service must be enabled with DLC characteristic. An example is displayed
below.

Local_1> SET PRIVILEGED
Local_1> system (not echoed)
Local_1>> SET SERVICE EPS_XXXXXX_P1 DLC ENABLED
Local_1>> SAVE SERVICE


Open the NT Print Manager; its icon is located in the Main window on the
desktop. Choose Create Printer from the Printer menu. In the dialog that
is displayed, enter the following:

 o Name of the queue on the NT host
 o A description string
 o Type of printer driver required

Note: If the printer driver isn't already installed, you'll need the
Windows NT installation disks.

If applicable, choose the Share this printer on the network option. (This
is not recommended until the print queue is confirmed to be running
properly.)

Click the Print To menu arrow to view its pull-down menu. Scroll to the
Network Printer option. From the dialog that appears, choose the Hewlett
Packard Network Port option and click the OK button.

Click the Card Address menu arrow to view its pull-down menu; all known
DLC nodes will be listed. Select the EPS' hardware address. Enter a name
to be associated with this address at the prompt. This printer name will
be displayed in the Print To menu mentioned above.

Click the Timers button. In the dialog that appears, click the Job Based
radio button. Click the OK button in each print dialog displayed.

If you'd like this printer to be the default printer, click the Default
menu arrow on the Create Printer window's title bar and scroll to the
printer's name.

3. Net BIOS Configuration ------------------------- To print with Net
BIOS protocol, a service must be with enabled with LanManager
characteristic. An example is displayed below:

Local_1> SET PRIVILEGED
Local_1> system (not echoed)
Local_1>> SET SERVICE EPS_XXXXXX_P1 LANMANAGER ENABLED
Local_1>> SAVE SERVICE

Choose one of the lpt ports to redirect (typically the ports to choose
from will be lpt1, lpt2, or lpt3.) Before you select a port to redirect,
ensure that it doesn't currently have a printer connected to it.

Double-click the DOS Command Prompt icon to open a DOS session. At the DOS
prompt, use the NET USE command to indicate that the lpt port will be
redirected to the EPS service [see example below].

C:> NET USE LPTn: \\EPS_XXXXXX\EPS_XXXXXX_P1

EPS_XXXXXX  = Print server's name
EPS_XXXXXX_P1  = Print service name on the print server

The Windows NT node will attempt to connect to the EPS. (If it cannot
connect to the EPS, it will inform you of the problem.) At this point, all
references to LPTn (the port specified with the NET USE command above)
will go to the service that you created. For testing purposes, you can try
to use a copy command, if the EPS is ready to accept data.

Exit the MS/DOS shell. Open the NT Print Manager; its icon is located in
the Main window on the desktop. Choose Create Printer from the Printer
menu.

In the dialog that is displayed, choose the following:

 o Name of the queue on the NT host
 o A description string
 o Type of printer driver required

Click the Print To menu arrow to view its pull-down menu, and select the
LPT port you wish to redirect. Click the OK button in each print dialog
displayed.

If you'd like this printer to be the default printer, click the Default
menu arrow on the Create Printer window's title bar and scroll to the
printer's name.

Note: Windows NT does not ship with a basic, text-only printer driver. If
you wish to print only to a terminal, you'll need to use some type of line
printer driver. and be willing to accept the embedded print codes.

================================================================================
            Title: Windows NT 4.0 Printing
           Author: Technical Support
     Date Created: 9/24/96
     Date Revised: 10/02/96
           Number: WINNT007
         Products: ETS/EPS/MPS/LPS
Hardware/Prom Rev:
 Software Version: 3.1/x and greater
      Host System: Microsoft
Operating systems: Windows NT 4.0 build 1381
     Network Type: Ethernet
      Approved by: Bruce
    Approved Date: 09/24/96
         Keywords: Windows NT, LPR, DLC, NetBIOS


Question:

How can I install Lantronix print servers with Windows NT 4.0?

Answer:

Lantronix print servers support the following printing methods in
Windows NT 4.0:

1. TCP/IP LPR Configuration:
---------------------------

The following installation assumes that TCP/IP protocol and printing
support has been installed on the Windows NT machine correctly. Please
refer to the Windows NT manuals or Microsoft Technical Support if you
are having difficulty.

The Lantronix print server will need to have a valid IP address
assigned prior to the print queue installation. There are several
methods which are described in the Installation guide. One of the most
common method to assign an IP address to an ethernet device is to use
the ARP command.

To use the ARP command, select the Command Prompt icon in the Programs
menu. In order to successfully assign IP addresses with Microsoft
TCP/IP stack, the local ARP table must have at least 1 entry other than
the local machine listed. To verify, type the following command:

ARP -A

To add entry to the ARP table, use the PING command to ping another IP
host on the same local network. For example:

PING 192.168.x.x

Once the ARP table has at least one entry in it, use the ARP -S
command to add the Lantronix print server's IP address to the ARP
table:

ARP -S XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX 00-80-A3-XX-XX-XX

XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX  = IP address to be assigned to print server
00-80-A3-XX-XX-XX  = Print server's Ethernet address

Next, ping the IP address to set the address on the server and verify
that it's alive.

PING XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX

This will temporarily set the IP address until the next time the server
is rebooted or power cycled.  To make this address permanent on the
print server, follow the instructions in the Installation guide.

To install an LPR print queue:

Select Settings then Printers from the Start menu or double click on
My Computer and then Printers

Double click on Add Printer

Select My Computer

Select Add Port

Select LPR Port

Provide input to the following:
	Name or address of server providing lpd: xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx
	Name of printer or print queue on that server:  eps_xxxxxx_p1


xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx = IP address of the Lantronix print server
eps_xxxxxx_p1 = name of print service on the print server

Select Close to finish adding network port

Select Next to chose the printer that is attached to this port

Change the printer name if needed and click on Next to continue

Select Shared and give the printer a shared name and client computers
that can print to this printer

Choose Yes to print a test page to the printer

The installation wizard will need to have the NT installation CD
available if the drivers are not already installed.



2. DLC Configuration:
---------------------

The following installation assumes that DLC protocol has been installed
on the Windows NT machine correctly. Please refer to the Windows NT
manuals or Microsoft Technical Support if you are having difficulty. In
addition,  DLC protocol is not supported with the LPS product.

To print with DLC protocol, a service on the print server must be
enabled with the DLC characteristic and the print server must have
LanManager protocol enabled . Follow the instructions in the
Installation manual regarding services characteristics.

An example is displayed below:

Local_1> SET PRIVILEGED
Local_1> system (not echoed)
Local_1>> SET SERVICE EPS_XXXXXX_P1 DLC ENABLED
Local_1>> SAVE SERVICE ALL

Select Settings then Printers from the Start menu or double click on
My Computer and then Printers

Double click on Add Printer

Select My Computer

Select Add Port

Select Hewlett-Packard Network Port and all new DLC devices should be
listed. Clicked on Refresh if the device is not there.

Select the correct print server' hardware address and provide a name to
be associated with this DLC port at the prompt.

Select the Timers button and choose the Job Based option.

Select Close to finish adding network port

Select Next to chose the printer that is attached to this port

Change the printer name if needed and click on Next to continue

Select Shared and give the printer a shared name and client computers
that can print to this printer

Choose Yes or No to print a test page to the printer then Finish

The installation wizard will need to have the NT installation CD
available if the drivers are not already installed.



3. NetBIOS/NetBEUI Configuration:
---------------------------------

The following installation assumes that NetBIOS/NetBEUI protocol has
been installed on the Windows NT machine correctly. Please refer to the
Windows NT manuals or Microsoft Technical Support if you are having
difficulty. In addition, LanManager printing is not supported with the
LPS product.

To print with NetBIOS/NetBEUI protocol, the print server must have
LanManager protocol enabled and a service on the print server enabled
with the LanManager characteristic. Follow the instructions in the
Installation manual regarding services characteristics.

An example is displayed below:

Local_1> SET PRIVILEGED
Local_1> system (not echoed)
Local_1>> SET SERVICE EPS_XXXXXX_P1 LANMANAGER ENABLED
Local_1>> SAVE SERVICE ALL

Determine one of the available lpt ports to redirect (typically the
ports to choose from will be lpt1, lpt2, or lpt3.)

Select the Command Prompt icon in the Programs menu or type Command in
the Run menu.

At the Command prompt, use the NET USE command to indicate that the lpt
port will be redirected to the Lantronix print server. For example:

NET USE LPT3: \\EPS_XXXXXX\EPS_XXXXXX_P1

EPS_XXXXXX  = Print server's name
EPS_XXXXXX_P1  = Print service name on the print server

The Windows NT node will attempt to connect to the print server. If it
cannot connect to the EPS, it will inform you of the problem. At this
point, all references to LPT3 (the port specified with the NET USE
command above) will go to the service that you created. For testing
purposes, you can try to use a copy command, if the printer is ready to
accept data.

Exit the Command prompt and Select Settings then Printers from the Start
menu or double click on My Computer and then Printers

Double click on Add Printer

Select My Computer

Select LPT3: as the available port and click on Next to chose the printer
that is attached to this port

Change the printer name if needed and click on Next to continue

Select Shared and give the printer a shared name and client computers
that can print to this printer

Choose Yes or No to print a test page to the printer then Finish

The installation wizard will need to have the NT installation CD
available if the drivers are not already installed.

