Joe Keane (jgk@jgk.org) wrote:
: In 1991, DEC had some impressive demos of the Alpha, and at the same
: time Apple was looking around for a new chip to replace the 68K in the
: Macintosh.  John Sculley met with Ken Olsen and proposed to re-design
: the Mac around the Alpha.  But Olsen thought that the Alpha wasn't going
: to fly; he liked the VAX and he wanted DEC to make some really souped-up
: VAXes, so he nixed the deal.  Needless to say, the Alpha people at DEC
: were not very impressed by this maneuver.

: Any comments on this?  Is it well known?

The "story" I heard from within Apple is that they considered all the
architectures and deals were discussed with all the players before going
with PowerPC. PowerPC was third or fourth on the list from a technical
point of view, but won for business reasons. The most notable of which was
IBM and Motorola guaranteed that they could deliver a million plus chips in
a certain timeframe. None of the other vendors were willing to comit to the
volumes in a timely fashion.

(The time frame for this would have been 1990 to early 1991. I don't think
Alpha was announced, though it was widely rumored. The R4000 was either
just announced and a year away from shipping or hadn't been announced at
all. Sun was soaking up pretty much all the SPARC chips that were being
produced and Apple would have had to compete with them for supply. On down
the line...)

That said, PowerPC is better for Macs than Alpha based on the
implementation history of each. Prior to the 21164PC (and somewhat still
after it) Alpha was fast but hot and expensive. DEC pretty much bailed on
doing anything about this when they bought into ARM and iced the low-power
Alpha project(s) in favor of StrongARM. (Probably a good decision business
wise.)

Architecturally, PowerPC has a few advantages for Macs as well. PowerPC
does not "require" a 64 bit datapath. PowerPC supports unaligned accesses
which make the 68k emulator (and quickly ported C code) work better. Lack
of 8/16 bit loads and stores in Alpha would have been very bad for an
interpreted 68k emulator and ported code too. (One could argue that DEC's
VAX->Alpha and MIPS->Alpha technology could have been repurposed for
68k. Its not clear to me that DEC's solution for that circa 1991/92 would
have worked in a mass market desktop platform.) The original Alpha
architecture specification did not support big-endian operation. Etc.

I still vote for MIPS as the best architecture in terms of suitable
implementations to go from top to bottom of the industry. From the smallest
ASIC cores to pretty respectable number crunchers. If DEC had done a high
performance MIPS-III implementation with VAX floating-point and Apple had
hopped on the MIPS bandwagon, things would be very different now. (For
example, you might be able to buy a single machine capable of running NT
and MacOS. Having only one alternative to the x86 for WindowsNT instead of
three would have greatly improved the chances of success for that
alternative.)

Actually, Apple/IBM/Motorola would have been fine if they hadn't totally
screwed the pooch on delivering a commodity computing platform. As in one
hardware spec that ran multiple OSes based on PowerPC. (Which still doesn't
exist in a buyable form. Despite all the hype about CHRP or whatever its
called today.) The result is that Apple is undergoing heat death, OS/2 is
somewhere between dead and a nop, and PowerPC is much less successful than
it could have been.

-Z-



