In article <5vOZNn9.d_braden@delphi.com>, d_braden@delphi.com wrote:
> I have an AlphaServer 1000 4/233 that is running OpenVMS 6.2 major rev 3, minor rev 0.
...
> This system has crashed 6 times since April 7 with a fatal bugcheck: INVEXCEPTN, Exception
> while above ASTDEL.  In the 6 months prior, this Alpha has crashed 10 times with the same
> bugcheck.  I have been copying the crash dumps off to tape but can't get DEC to analyze them
> since we do not have DEC software maintenance of any sort.

You are going to have to learn your way around SDA and learn how to interpret
crash dumps, at least to determine which module is responsible. The stack will
show addresses of modules being executed and, as you go down the stack, modules
that either called or were interrupted by the modules higher on the stack.

In your case you'll see an exception handler at the bottom of the stack
(executing a bugcheck instruction once it figured out it had a serious
exception) and, higher up the stack, an exception frame from some exception,
perhaps an ACCVIO or something.  The PC of the exception will tell you where
it was, and from interpreting the instructions, why it went wrong.
 
CLUE CRASH and CLUE STACK at the SDA prompt (or SHOW CRASH and SHOW STACK)
are a start to see what was going on.  SDA makes a best effort at interpreting
addresses and values, but often gets it wrong.

>  The one piece of SDA info that
> is consistent between all of these crashs is that the process name is always "NULL" and
> process id is always "00010000".

This is a red herring.  You are seeing a crash at high IPL and the current
process is often meaningless, especially if it's the NULL process.  The code
thread is likely started from an interrupt, such as a driver or a software
interrupt.

What is running on the system?  Third party drivers?  Any "interesting"
layered products, particularly those with drivers or similar?  Any Digital
patches installed?

This is the barest start, there is a lot to learn in interpreting crash dumps.
Particularly Alpha.  Have fun!

-Mike



