The 3100 SCSI card has 3 connectors on the top. One connects to the
motherboard. The other two connect to SCSI cables; one is typically
used to connect to a loop that has 2 to 4 50 bin square SCSI connectors
on it. Because it loops all the way round, no terminator is there. The
other connects to an external SCSI connector; any cable that will do
this will work. In principle you can connect either connector this
way and just terminate the cable at the far end.

This card does only asynchronous SCSI, but will support several devices
and seems to be the same across the whole older 3100 line. Recent vax
3100s have hopefully more modern SCSI, but these do work with lots of
devices.

What I describe exists in VS3100/30, /40, /38, /48, and /76. Hopefully
someone will answer with more info.



