Well,

Since you asked, the 60 and 66MHz DEC Pentium PCs have 5 volt Socket 4
processor sockets, and a Pentium 133 is a Socket 5, while a Pentium
166 or 200 is a Socket 7.  If you can figure out some reliable and
less expensive way to plug a dirt cheap Pentium 133 into a Socket 4,
you will have accomplished something.  What we're selling does just
that, at a price attractive enough (we think) that owners of these
systems may want to get more life out of them rather than replacing
them.

And, since you asked, the DECpc XL 560 and 566 use a DEC-proprietary
motherboard/daughthercard arrangement, and the motherboard would have
to be replaced to use a daughtercard with a faster Pentium.

Any more questions????   Ben Myers

