is evaporating.  For instance, here at Caltech, VMS machines are being
phased out left and right, mostly being replaced by Unix workstations, only
a fraction of which are from Digital, but also by PCs, none of which are
from Digital.  Digital revenues are down 10% from last year, from sales 
around here I'd wager that small and medium machine sales are down at least 
50%.  This strategy is so short sighted that it is painful to watch.  All of
those Caltech students who used to use a VMS cluster for their mail and
other general tasks now log onto Suns instead.  They don't know OpenVMS
from a hole in the ground, and you can bet they will not be ordering those
precious turbolasers when they buy hardware for their companies years from
now. 

I'd wager serious money that every one of those 120 "new" OpenVMS sales was
instigated by somebody in the company with previous OpenVMS experience,
even if the company itself had no OpenVMS machines at the time. "Customer"
is not the name on the masthead, "customer" is a person, somewhere in an
organization who determines which machine will be purchased.  Digital is
wiping out (and pissing off) the pool of people who would make a "buy
Digital" decision, and thus pouring fuel onto the fire of their continuing
decline. 

