jonathan@rubens.its.unimelb.edu.au (Jonathan Ridler) wrote:

Jonathan,

Two paragraphs of your original letter to Palmer deserve some comment
from a long-time professional (me) with many years inside a computer
manufacturer much like Digital, except much smaller.  Never worked for
Digital in my life, but know and speak to a lot of Digital employees
in the neighborhood, literally at the nexus of Digital here in
Massachusetts... 

>Digital should make OpenVMS available on every platform that it sells, whether
>it be a top-end server or an entry-level desktop.  There appear to be no
>technical reasons why this should not happen - the decision seems based on the
>same flawed marketing reasoning which has led to such a decline in OpenVMS
>support.

There are probably no technical reasons why OpenVMS should not be
available on every platform sold by Digital.  But there are a lot of
other business reasons... budgetary including staffing, hardware and
other resources required to make this happen.  The technical reasons
are only a fraction of the total picture.  Digital sets budgets based
on its best estimate of how it can get the most bang for its buck,
i.e. sell the most computers for the amount of money spent on
research, development, manufacturing, quality assurance, marketing,
advertising, etc. etc. etc.  Can you suggest a better way to allocate
budget?

If OpenVMS runs or will run on less than the full range of Digital
hardware platforms, rest assured there is a good business basis for
this.

>Digital should not oppose, and may even want to encourage the development of a
>freeware version of OpenVMS, or possibly even a migration to Intel if
>technically achievable.

Do you seriously think that someone would be capable of writing an
OpenVMS clone from the ground up based on public Digital documents,
like Linux was written?

The only way for a freeware version of OpenVMS to ever happen would be
for Digital to release the entire source code and supporting
engineering documents of OpenVMS to the public domain.  

But even if this happened, I have serious doubt that a Linus Torvalds
(forgive me if I misspelled his name), or a Red Hat or some other
organization would step forward and take ownership for the
distribution of such a beast.  

Since OpenVMS would run on mostly on Digital hardware until the Intel
version got running, Digital Field Service, 3rd party service
organizations, and IT professionals would have a helluva mess on their
hands trying to figure out what was broken, hardware or freeware, and
whom to call to fix it.

Put yourself in the shoes of the director of IT running business
critical computer hardware and software.  Would you like to be that
person when the freeware broke, and all systems ground to a halt?  How
would you explain to his boss, the company president, that the company
business stopped dead in its tracks because the freeware broke?

Frankly, I think the idea of a freeware OpenVMS is an unrealistic one
outside of academic and software research circles.

Seems like your real issue with OpenVMS is pricing.  Either Digital
brings OpenVMS software pricing within the same order of magnitude as
Windows NT and UNIX, or the marketplace may decide to exclude OpenVMS
from many of its long-term buying decisions.  Let Digital figure out
the best pricing and hope for the best since you have so much at stake
in OpenVMS.  And encourage them to do better software pricing rather
than bashing them for current pricing... Ben Myers 




