Hmmmmm.. I worked at Convex (and DEC)..... 

Steve Wallach likes to tell a story about deciding on an operating 
system for the first Convex machine in the mid-80's.  He went first
to DEC to use VMS because most of the scientific community loved it. 
DEC laughed at him.  Convex then went to Berkely, gave them $50K, and
then received a tape in the mail for a whole Unix production system. 
Picking Unix was pure luck.... 

The point is.... Pricing makes a platform ubiquitous.... Steve points
out that DEC could still have owned all of the scientific community 
had they been willing to free-up VMS.  Sun understood this back then, 
and they ate Digital's lunch by making NFS, yellow pages, and other 
software freely available.  DEC tried with X-Windows but could not 
define an effective business model to work with it. 

Software is a drug... You hook a company by giving them free software...
Then, they need to get on the nine-step program to get off... 

Heck, it is difficult to work within this business model.  As you can
see, Convex went out off business (bought by HP) because it could not 
adapt to the decline of the supercomputer. 



