In article <01bc509c$f0aed080$b20b100a@0000194435>, "Andy Back" <andyb@liii.com> writes:

:Just a few questions about the DEC PCi33 M/B (166MHz, AT style KB)...

   I assume you have an AXPpci33 motherboard.

   The AXPpci33 supported by Windows NT 3.5 and 3.51, Digital Unix 3.0B,
   3.2, and 4.0, and Linux, and probably a few other release versions and
   operating systems.  In the case of the DIGITAL operating systems, the
   Software Product Descriptions will contain details.  In the case of
   NT, check the HCL.  As for Linux, donno...

   OpenVMS does not support this particular configuration, and OpenVMS
   only recently added support for certain systems -- specifically, the
   AlphaBOOK -- based on the DECchip 21066 Alpha microprocessor used in
   this board.

   The Alpha 21064/21064A PCI reference board (EB64+), the Alpha 21164
   PCI reference board (EB164), and the Alpha PC64 reference board (APC64)
   are all supported by the current OpenVMS release.  The AXPpci 233XL
   should also bootstrap OpenVMS.  (In addition to the motherboard, all
   other I/O widgets must be recognized and supported, of course...)

   Getting an operating system to run on a new platform means that one
   must adapt the source code to the configuration of the new platform,
   or the platform must closely emulate a previous -- and currently
   supported -- system.  (And the AXPpci33 does not emulate any other
   platform currently supported by OpenVMS.)

:And does anyone know if the ATI Mach64 card requires 3.3v (PCI)?

   You might want to ask the manufacturer.

