In comp.os.vms Ben Myers <benmyers@ma.ultranet.com> wrote:
> Do you seriously think that someone would be capable of writing an
> OpenVMS clone from the ground up based on public Digital documents,
> like Linux was written?
>
> The only way for a freeware version of OpenVMS to ever happen would be
> for Digital to release the entire source code and supporting
> engineering documents of OpenVMS to the public domain.  

  It depends on what you mean by an "OpenVMS clone" - the following things
have already been done:

  DCL (VCL, Boston Business Computing)
  BACKUP (VBACKUP, also Boston Business Computing)
  EDT (EDT+, also Boston Business Computing)
  TPU (Nu/TPU?)
  VAX BASIC (shareware for PC-DOS, a long time ago)
  DCL + some system environment stuff (Wendin/DOS, also a long time ago)
  DEC Notes (WebNotes/NetNotes, OS Technologies [now Spyglass])
  Probably lots of others I've forgotten or missed

  Now, lots of that is commercial stuff - but the point is, it *is* possible
to do it. I suspect that nobody cares enough to get such a project going,
because VMS isn't the desirable environment it once was. Also, using such an
environment would require at least some code changes, so users might just
decide to port from VMS to some other platform instead.

