>not have such effects with the DEC3000/300X I used before, but I thought
>"Well, you don't reboot every day" and smiled.  I also smiled about the
>fact that xmcd (a popular Audio-CD-player software) needed a few minutes to
>log in a new CD.
>
>Two weeks ago, we made an update to DU 4.0B .  I was told that DU4.0 contained
>better support for non-DEC SCSI devices, and I was hoping to get rid of these
>annoying side-effects.  The result was:
>
>The DU 4.0D ISP driver does not only print timeout messages for the SCSI
>streamer, it dies with a kernel panic after the first message!  I have to boot
>with the streamer turned off to get my system up!  xmcd's behaviour has also
>become worse: If I start xmcd and there is currently no CD in the drive, the
>drive becomes completely "locked": any further access, regardless if I want to
>mount a data CDROM or play an Audio CD returns a "no such device" message 
though
>the drive is still there and a CD is inserted!  I have to reboot the machine 
to 
>make the CD-ROM accessible again!
>

I'm assuming that the cable that you are using coming out of the back of the 
AlphaStation 500 is a wide-to-narrow adapter cable that terminates the high 
order bits of wide SCSI, right?

The AS 500 has fast, wide SCSI coming out the back.  If you are using a narrow 
device (like your tape drive), you can't just use an adapter cable that ignores 
the high 8 bits of wide SCSI and just passes thru the low order bits.  The 
upper 8 bits must be terminated.

Digital does offer a cable (bn36A-0B, I think) that does this.

Also, what's the length of cable, etc, and is the SCSI bus correctly 
terminated?

The problem sounds like a SCSI problem, not an OS or software issue...

Mark
