On 22 Apr 1997 gmac63@perigee.net wrote:
> We have been looking at an SGI Indigo runnign UNIX, 
> But the cost is outrageous, Not to mention we would like to keep on running
> Windows NT Server. What are the advantages of a DEC Alpha workstation/server
> over the Wintel boxes and can the DEC Machines achieve the raw power the
> SGIs can?

  As far as the advantages of DEC over Intel, well, it basically comes down
to: The Alpha is a fairly new architecture that DEC took the time to do right
the first time around (for the most part).  Intel is one kludge on top of
another, patched and fixed from here to there and back.  This is not anyone's
fault, but it is the way things are.  The Alpha is also a fully 64-bit RISC
machine, while the Intel is generally CISC and generally 32-bit, with a few
16-bit things that just won't seem to go away. 

  The practical upshot of all this is, a good Alpha will run quite a bit
faster then an Intel, even at the same clock speeds.  Intels also top out a
lot sooner then Alphas do -- DEC sells some *really* nice super-high-end
Alphas, but that really doesn't apply here.  Price/performance is another
issue (that I don't want to get into), but if you're looking for a single
powerful box, Alpha generally wins, hands down.

  As far as the whole NT/Unix thing, well, if you're used to NT and you like
NT, then keep NT.  Go with a good DEC box (or good Alpha clone box) running NT
4.0.  It should blow away anything Intel can come up with.  Unix is a wholly
different beast, and while people may argue the points of one vs the other,
sticking with what you know counts for a lot.  :-) 

  Incidentally, above-mentioned DEC super-high-end stuff generally does beat
SGI for things like file servers and database servers.  SGI tends to lead in
graphics, for obvious reasons.  YMMV, I may be wrong, the usually disclaimers
all apply.  :-) 

