>I'm assuming that the cable that you are using coming out of the back of the 
>AlphaStation 500 is a wide-to-narrow adapter cable that terminates the high 
>order bits of wide SCSI, right?

>The AS 500 has fast, wide SCSI coming out the back.  If you are using a narrow 
>device (like your tape drive), you can't just use an adapter cable that ignores 
>the high 8 bits of wide SCSI and just passes thru the low order bits.  The 
>upper 8 bits must be terminated.

>Digital does offer a cable (bn36A-0B, I think) that does this.

>Also, what's the length of cable, etc, and is the SCSI bus correctly 
>terminated?

>The problem sounds like a SCSI problem, not an OS or software issue...

I have a wide SCSI-->Centronics adapter directly attached to the external SCSI
connector that terminates the upper eight bits.  The cable to the external box
is about one meter long and the terminator directly sits on the other plug of
the external box.  I heavily doubt that I have a termination problem.  The
problems are too reproducable and a faulty termination would also have an effect
on the internal drives (I once ripped out the external terminator accidentally
and the SCSI bus almost immediately froze...).

I would be happy with a way to tell the ISP driver not to probe other LUNs than
0, and I hoped that the NVRAM has such a flag.  But as I see only a hexdump of
128 bytes, I would have to test about 128*8 bits wiyhout documentation...

