Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.hardware,comp.sys.dec


Actually, most of the newer drives we install have a jumper that
controls spin-up. In a system with lots of RAM and many drives, we've
had to stagger spin-up to allow everything to initialize without sucking
too many amps. Staggered spin is generally arbitrated by SCSI id, so
drive 0 spins up first, followed by id 1, etc.

Paul Thompson wrote:
> 
> Spin up on the older DEC RZ drives is done via software, unless you set a
> jumper on the drive.  I know this question has come up several times
> before so I believe it might be a FAQ question perhaps in the Ultrix FAQ so
> check that first to find out specifically which jumper.
> 
> I do not believe that most other SCSI drives have this "feature".
> 
> Paul
> 
> In article <01bc4ff2$82626e20$964c18d2@klondike>,
> t"Ben Hantz" <klondike@pacific.net.sg> writes:
