Organization: The Destek Group, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


If there is an unrecoverable cpu error, Digital UNIX will crash the
system and reboot.  The hardware test will note the cpu is no longer
usable, and the system will boot without it, even if it is cpu 0.  The
fundamental problem is there is data in the cache of that failed
processor that is not replicated anywhere.  Also that processor may
have been in a critical region of the operating system so merely
removing it from the system can lead to correctness problems.  I don't
know how other systems deal with these problems.  Perhaps there are
straight-forward solutions to these problems, perhaps not.  Perhaps
they limit the generality of their solution.  But I do know that 
this is what Digital UNIX does.

