>This would probably be true if an appliation was compute intensive and
>it was optimized to use SMP very well.  However, if there was lots of
>memory access, the PPros would quickly become starved due to the slower
>memory access, and the Alpha would win.

	Unfortunately, most apps are written to take advantage of
	single CPU systems and so a single app cannot really take
	advantage of an SMP;  You'd need to run something like Unix
	(or it's deviants) and run multiple processes/threads to
	get anywhere near the full bandwidth of the box.  For a single
	user (except for a geek like me) an SMP is a waste.  For
	multi-abuser systems, though, they can pay off.

	The Alpha (and it's friends, like the UltraSparc) has extremely
	high performance in a single processor, so it can provide more
	single-thread bandwidth (not quite mainframe class, but let's
	not fantasize too much).  Managing a single processor, BTW, is
	far simpler than dealing w/ multiple processors.  Usually, an
	SMP 2X will seldom get above 1.4X the processing power of a
	1X since there's all kinds of mutexes and bus accesses that
	the competing systems must deal with (Sperry-UNIVAC got around
	this by using a single multipoort cache unit to feed up to
	2 processors, giving them a 1.8X on a 2by).

	So, a single Alpha, running at approx 2X the MIPS of a PPro
	will *definately* beat 2 PPros.  The performance of an SMP
	is non-linear.  And this assumes multi-threaded apps that
	can keep more than one processor busy.

