	Once again somebody is comparing apples to wintels (sorry, I
	couldn't resist this).

	2 PPros != 1 Alpha;  The Alpha would stomp them both flat.

	Look at the desktop;  If someone (say, a scientist) needs to
	do serious number crunching on a commercially available spread-
	sheet, he's gonna waste less time waiting for results if he's
	running on an Alpha since the app wouldn't even warm up the
	second PPro in an SMP (actually, a rational system will cause
	a process to "walk" across the CPUs in order to exercise the
	paths, but only one CPU in the system will really be working).

	Now if this researcher has written an app that is multi-
	threaded (thus taking advantage of potential SMP) he *still*
	comes out ahead on an Alpha since the 2xPPro is only (AFAIK)
	1.4-1.5X a 1xPPro.

	There *are* places an SMP is useful.  The desktop ain't one
	of them.  If you are going SMP, go SMP with an Alpha-  sure
	it's expensive (until the 21164PC comes out) but you'll *love*
	the number of cycles you have to work with.

	The problem w/ the Pentium and PPro is that it's still held
	back by the core processor model-  an 8080.  It's like IBM
	w/ the 360/370 architecture-  a byte oriented machine.  Any
	technology that gives a boost to a byte oriented machine will
	give a *bigger* boost to a word-oriented machine.  Sure, there
	are coding caveats, but do you really need to worry about these
	anymore?  Still using R13 to point to a savearea?

	Me, Opinionated?	YES!
	Me, Correct?		MMMMM....	Maybe!

	I've been in this field so long I remember when computer graphics
	was something you got out of the card punch then held up to the
	light...

