I don't really know, but we had a 4100 4 CPU box delivered with 
one faulty CPU.  The machine frequently crashed, and analysis of
the crash dumps indicated (sometimes) that the kernel had panicked
as a result of something going on on CPU 1.
It never went three-quarters down.

Is the kernel not multi-threaded?  If so, then bits of the OS will
be running on any processor, and if one goes then the thing will
surely crash.  Even if not, important user-level root processes
are likely to be scattered around different CPUs, and I imagine
the effect of these dying could easily be fatal.

My impression was that the SRM console program ran on CPU 0 
(or wherever) but apart from that they were all equal.
But note I'm not any kind of DEC expert.  There are no doubt people
out there more informed than me who will reply.

