The USENET cabal made Curt Sampson <cjs@cynic.portal.ca> say:
>In article <Pine.SGI.3.95.970424020431.10748H-100000@leonardo.sr.unh.edu>,
>Ben Scott  <bscott@leonardo.sr.unh.edu> wrote:
>
>>  The practical upshot of all this is, a good Alpha will run quite a bit
>>faster then an Intel, even at the same clock speeds.
>
>This is not true. For ballpark figures for integer performance,
>you can compare a 166 MHz 21064 to a 90 MHz or 100 MHz Pentium,

In the case of the UDB, this is true.  However, the UDB is also handicapped
by its narrow memory bus and miniscule cache.  A 166 MHz 21064 with a
decent memory bus and cache would be significantly faster than the 166 UDBs.
It probably wouldn't outpace a Pentium/166 (unless the Pentium was
handicapped by a slow memory bus or something similar), but it might beat a
486/166 (Does such a beast exist?).

The processor's MHz rating is _not_ the prime determining factor of a
processor's speed.  Memory size, cache size, and memory busses are very
important and are getting more important as the memory/processor speed
balance becomes even more exaggerated.  Furthermore, the differing
instruction sets, pipelining, and superscalarity of different processors
make any comparison between the speeds of processors a conditional one.
You can say, "Processor X runs [insert favorite benchmark here] faster
than processor Y."  You cannot extrapolate from that statement that
processor X is _always_ faster than processor Y.  It depends on the
application.

I guess many people in this newsgroup know this, but I wanted to try to
clear up a little confusion (and beat the dead equine a bit more.)

-andy "just sat through CS345, Computer Architecture, and proud of it."
