In article <slrn5lv3id.ijp.adisaacs@icu15.csl.mtu.edu>,
Andy Isaacson <adisaacs@mtu.edu> wrote:

>The USENET cabal made Curt Sampson <cjs@cynic.portal.ca> say:
>>
>>This is not true. For ballpark figures for integer performance,
>>you can compare a 166 MHz 21064 to a 90 MHz or 100 MHz Pentium,
>
>In the case of the UDB, this is true.  However, the UDB is also handicapped
>by its narrow memory bus and miniscule cache.

Yes, I'm well aware of the limitations of the UDB. However, the
dhrystone benchmark I used was small, and appears to have fit
entirely into on-chip first-level cache. I got almost exactly the
same performance figures from a P133 and a 233 MHz 21066.

From my examination of the SPECint95 ratings for various machines,
I believe that my results are not unusual. Keep in mind that if
any of the following systems have an advantage due to memory
configuration or cache size, it would be the Alphas, since it is
rather difficult to find a Pentium system with a 128-bit memory
bus with two-way interleave and 8 MB of second-level cache.

CINT95
Company           System                         Baseline   Result
Dell Computer Co  Dell Dimension XPS Pro180n (150M  6.25     6.25
Dell Computer Co  Dell Dimension XPS Pro180n (180M  7.28     7.28
Dell Computer Co  Dell Dimension XPS Pro200n        8.20     8.20
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 500/266              6.53     7.93
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 500/333              8.38     9.82
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 500/400              9.77    12.3
Digital Equipmen  AlphaStation 500/500             12.6     15.0

(These figures come from
<http://www.specbench.org/osg/cpu95/results/cint95.html>.)

You will note that even when you look at the result (the peak value)
rather than the base, a 266 MHz 21164 comes out slower than a 200
MHz Pentium Pro.

So I still maintain that, as a rough rule of thumb, to match or
exceed the integer performance of a Pentium/Pentium Pro running at
x MHz, you need a 2016x/2116x running at 1.3x to 1.5x MHz.

