> chip.  I am planning on running either Linux 4.0 or Windows NT. I understand
> the OS binary will be different from Intel base computer. but what about
> applications? Are they bianry compatiable?

No, they aren't binary compatible, but read on...

> Let's say I got httpd for Linux
> from NCSA, would the binary run on my Linux based alpha?

In general, whenever there _is_ a native Alpha binary, you'll want to
run the native version.  Now, Red Hat 4.x comes with the Apache http
server, so you might want to run it instead of the NCSA server.  If
that doesn't suit your needs, you could run it through em86 (actually,
once you install em86, you can run x86 binaries as if they were native
binaries---the only difference is that they'll be quite a bit slower).

For more info on em86, see ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/em86.

Unfortunately, a 166MHz UDB is rather slow so I'm not sure how much
joy you'll have with em86.  On a 300MHz+ ev5-based machine, em86 is
quite snappy though (definitively good enough to run the Linux/x86
binary of Netscape, etc.).

Hope this helps,

