In article <1997Apr21.140740.748@tachyon.com>, wayne@tachyon.com-kill-spam-it-sux (Wayne Sewell) writes:
> 
> ... This thread is about dumb terminals.  The original poster thinks they
> are wonderful.  I think they are obsolete.  The existence of smart terminals, x
> terminals, etc. has nothing to do with that.  In fact, it underscores the fact
> that dumb terminals are obsolete.

Perhaps.

However, there are still niches in which dumb terminals are not only
useful, but are superior to smart terminals or PCs.  Case in point:

We have an MRP-II system which runs on RS/6000 boxes; it is specifically
intended for use with character-cell workstations with VT200 emulation.
Our usage of these systems falls into two general classes -- workstations
which do nothing but MRP-II interaction and which do it continuously
during the day, and workstations which need occasional access to MRP-II
data.

For the first category, about 75% of all MRP-II workstations, we
evaluated several options and selected dumb terminals (VT510s) and
now have around 250 of them in service in a dozen plants in the US
and Mexico, and plan to install about another 100 this year.  The
reasons for this apparently strange choice (and it did take some
selling internally) were:

  * Cost.  In quantity, we have been consistently able to get new
    VT510s for around $400 with the DEC warranty and $350 with a
    distributor's warranty.  Yes, we have tons of 286 and 386 PCs
    lying around unused -- but the cost of refurbing one of these
    (adding memory, replacing the graphics card (usually moved to
    the newer computer), adding a monitor (also moved forward), a
    new keyboard (nearly always), and a TCP/IP-terminal-emulation
    license) costs us a little more that $450 on average.  For an
    old workstation, capable of running only now-unsupported versions
    of the 'operating system' and software, with poor reliability
    and poorer parts availability.

  * Legibility.  Our MRP-II systems generates many 132-column
    reports.  Even when a 132-column graphics card  is available
    (at extra $$), the display on a dot-matrix screen is nearly
    illegible.

  * Reliability.  Of around 250 VT510s installed so far, we have
    had two DOA and one power switch failure in service.  Some units
    have been in service for four years now.  (Oh, yes.  Five keyboards
    replaced -- one was crushed by a skidload of something falling
    off a fork-lift truck and four were damaged by unspecified liquids.)
    Most of the terminals are installed in manufacturing areas in our
    production plants, in environments where PCs tend to have a short
    life (in our worst-case plant, I have seldom seen a PC run for
    more than a year, even when de-dusted weekly).  The VT510s have
    ridden through power surges which have damaged power supplies in
    PCs and printers plugged into the same quad outlet (Mexican utility
    power, at least in the areas of our plants, is a sore trial).

  * Security.  You won't find operators playing Doom on a terminal.
    Nor can the network be infected by a virus from a terminal. 

> The original poster did not say anything about a having a printer.
> Besides, can you do a search of the screen capture?  It is often
> useful to scan log files for various strings.  A computer can do
> this by capturing to a file, a dumb terminal can't.

True.  However, the application which these terminals run provides
these functions in an easily accessible form (function keys for screen
printing, for example).  Yes, it takes a computer to to these things,
but the computer need not necessarily be part of the display device.

Mark Shumaker
shumaker@eisner.decus.org



