In article <5ja1sk$8nb$1@rzsun02.rrz.uni-hamburg.de>,
>I'm moving from a VAXStation 3100 M76 with VMS 5.2 which I've
>always accessed with VT (clones---Ampex, Falco, Pericom) terminals to an
>Alpha with VMS 7.1 and 17" colour monitor with DECWindows.  The years of
>terminal work have set high standards for what I expect from a terminal
>emulation, 

I hadt he same experience.  The one thing I found MOST disconcerting about
the transition to DECterm emulators, was that the DECterm ITSELF buffers
the data sent to it for display, but passes ^Y through to the host rather
than acting on it ITSELF as might sometimes be useful: if you've got a
long scrolling display (say a DUMP of a long file) flying past, and you
decide you want to interrupt the operation with ^Y, you have to wait for
the DECterm to empty its output buffer to the screen before the ^Y takes
effect.  If you intended to interrupt the output for purposes of leaving
something on the screen for detailed examination, either you have to
scroll back to it (handy, I admit, but not the solution to all problems)
OR it's so far gone that it is no longer in the scrollback buffer.  The
longer you wait initially, before pressing ^Y, the more stuff is in the
output buffer and the longer you have to wait for the results of your ^Y
to reach the display.  Digital folks, would it be possible to either
provide a "clear the output buffer" keystroke (NOT just a
pulldown-menu-option!  Those are too slow!), OR tie that functionality
into ^Y somehow?  When I hit ^Y, I want the display to STOP RIGHT THERE --
NOT ten pages later when the buffer empties.

>keys send what they're supposed to (even after being redefined, which,
>however, was rarely needed on a good clone) which, at least with UNIX,
>is a nightmare under X.

It is a little easier to redefine certain keys -- those Digital
feels are the most commonly useful to redefine, I assume -- in a
DECterm.  But the underlying X nightmare is still there if you
want it!  (And can get to it; Digital doesn't provide many of the 
"standard" Unix X utilities AFAIK...)


>hope I won't miss the escape key, the backspace and linefeed keys (the

For escape you can type ^[ (control right-square-bracket), for backspace
^H, and for linefeed ^J.  It's a little awkward but you get used to it...

>have to get used to the name of the key not being printed on it,

Maybe someone sells adhesive keycap labels?  Maybe -I- should!

>wondering if it is possible to reconfigure certain keys so that they
>ALWAYS send another value.

It is probably possible to do what you want to do, but I think you're
going to have to mess with that underlying "nightmare" to do it.  

>  I don't mean like F11-F13 which VMS
>sometimes interprets as ESC, BS and LF (at least that's how I understand
>it)

Depending on one's terminal (or emulator), F11-F13 can be configured to
transmit actual ESC, BS, and LF codes down a serial line or into the
keyboard interface of one's workstation -- but it appears from a quick
test here that the default is for F11-F13 to transmit escape sequences 
just as do the rest of F6-F20.

This leads to some interesting observations.

- A small program that reads individual interactive keystrokes "from the
  terminal" and displays their ASCII codes, clearly shows the escape
  sequences arising from pressing F7-F20.  

- F6 is a special case, because the terminal driver recognizes the escape
  sequence from that key and treats it "like Control-Y" -- SORT OF.  I
  have an application which intercepts control-C and control-Y -- but
  I can still "instantly interrupt" it by pressing F6.

- The terminal driver's interpretation of F6-as-control-Y can happen
  "belatedly," if the escape sequence is typed manually.  The sequence
  is "<ESC>[17~", and if I type those characters individually to my
  ASCII-echoing program, the <ESC>, [, 1, and 7 are echoed individually
  -- and then when the final ~ is struck, the terminal driver "suddenly
  realizes" that this was "an F6 keystroke" and DCL's ^Y handler fires
  and interrupts my program.  (Interestingly, if I then issue a CONTINUE
  command and resume execution of the program, a 7 is echoed -- the same
  7 from the F6 keystroke, "again," or an independent 7 coming from 
  somewhere else, I know not.)

- In EDT's "screen" editing mode, F12 and F13 cause behavior identical to
  "real" BS and LF strokes, as long as you're in the actual editing
  window.  At the "command:" prompt brought up by GOLD KP7, however, F12
  appear to be taken literally and cause errors.  F11 definitely does 
  NOT behave as ESC in the EDT screen editor at ANY time.  I can see why
  EDT's designers would do that, but I'm not sure I could put it into
  words at the moment!


>I'm looking for a way to do it ONCE to make it
>valid EVERYWHERE, like on a terminal, and not redo it in each
>application.  (Of course, if some application interprets these in a non-
>ASCII way, that's a different matter of no concern at the moment.) 

Some low-level part of X (perhaps, but not necessarily, uniquely part of
the X server) has the job of mapping arbitrary keyboards' idiosyncratic
output (which is NOT ASCII -- just a series of keypress and key-release
signals) to the defined set of Xlib keypress and key-release events.  It
is then the job of the individual application to decide what use to make
of these events, whether that be to insert the appropriate character(s)
into a pseudoterminal's input stream, steer a videogame spaceship, or
what-have-you. 

(Which reminds me -- Digital, I notice that I can accidentally command the
Window Manager to close a DECterm window but that this does NOT always
cause termination of the associated interactive login process.  Is there
any capability in DECterm, DECW$TERMINAL, etc., for creating a new
terminal window and "reattaching" it to the old/existing pseudoterminal
and the session logged in on it?  That would sure be handy.)

>Specifically, I would like the key to the left of 1/! to be `/~ (
>probably the default) and not ESC.  ESC should be moved to the </> which 
>is to the left of z/Z (< and > are SHIFT , and . so they're redundant 
>here).  

It is possible to swap SOME of those keys around in DECterm, but not ALL
of them as far as I can tell.  Now that I think about it, I did have to
change from the "out of the box" defaults to a couple of custom settings,
in order to get "<" and ">" onto the "," and "." keys.  I think at one
point I had it so badly misconfigured that I DIDN'T HAVE "<" and ">" AT
ALL -- or at least was missing ONE of them.

>The ALT on the right should be LF and the COMPOSE on the right 
>should be BS (I'm assuming that the ALT and COMPOSE on the left will 
>give me all the functionality I need.

I'm not sure you CAN intercept COMPOSE and ALT by themselves; they MAY be
acting within the keyboard hardware itself, to simply cause OTHER keys to
send different signals than "normally."  I could be wrong, though.

Hopefully this message contains SOME useful information...

Chris Chiesa
