>>[Things I have to admit are significant advantages to using a
>>workstation instead of a VT100, elided for the sake of brevity]
> 
> I don't think anyone would deny the advantages of being able to transfer
> files, etc. -- however, I can do _most_ of that with an Atari 800 that
> takes maybe 20 seconds to boot into a full-functioned 80-column terminal
> emulator with Kermit file-transfer and the best VT100 emulation I've ever
> seen (beats all hell out of anything I've ever seen for the Mac or the PC)
> -- and that's when booting from FLOPPY disk.  Boot from a HD and you're
> talking maybe 5 seconds 'til you're up and online.  

This is one of the strangest threads I have ever seen.  The topic is about the
utility, or lack thereof, of dumb terminals.  Yet everyone responds with a
discussion of *smart* terminals: personal computers running emulators,
x-terminals, and the like.

You are really agreeing with me.  I maintain that a computer, or at least a
smart terminal, is more useful than a dumb terminal for accessing remote sites
as the original poster was doing.  Since you are using a computer, none of your
comments above dispute that.

>The point is that the
> incredibly bloated infrastructure required for "today's version" of most
> of the points you cited, ISN'T a NECESSITY; it's just something you (and a
> lot of other people) have been duped into THINKING is a necessity.  It's a
> "given" of life that most things that are done in ANY area, computers and
> everything else, could be done a LOT BETTER, period.

Well, of course it's not a necessity, in the sense that I won't fall over and
die without it.  Computers themselves aren't a necessity by that standard. 
However, using a workstation does permit me to do more work in the same amount
of time and to reduce the connect time (remember, the original post was about
remotely accessing a computer with a vt100 and a modem).  Both of these result
in less cost to the customer, who is paying by the hour for me and by the
minute for the phone connection.  I can do work offline in between connects and
I can do background work locally while connected.  For instance, I can access
vms help or the cdrom doc set in another window on my machine, rather than
doing it on the remote machine over a possibly slow modem link.  The guy with
the vt100 can't do that.

This whole subthread came about because he thought that using a vt100 for
remote connection is better.  I dispute that.  I concede only that it is
simpler and cheaper.

>>The dumb terminal belongs on the trash heap of history, rubbing shoulders with
>>the buggy whip, the 8-track tape, and leisure suits.
> 
> Well, the buggy whip will be back when we run out of gasoline (any time
> now, according to some people), the 8-track tape still plays music if you
> didn't play lemming and throw out your player (I still have mine) 

If I still had mine, it would be painful to listen to after CD.

>-- and
> ALL fashions come back into style every 20 years or so -- ask any
> middle-aged woman.

Maybe not all.  The Nehru jacket appeared (briefly) around 68 or 69.  Their
reappearance is nearly ten years beyond schedule.

Wayne
