On Mon, 21 Apr 1997 14:20:51 GMT, Michael Heller
<mheller@eXtraActive.net> wrote:

>> 
>> All that I was saying was for ten grand, I could build a machine that
>> would be comparable to that 28 grand Alpha. Even though the Alpha has a
>> better FPU unit, a dual PPro would be close to that power, and a quad
>> ought to surpass it.
>
>You shouldn't rely so heavily on spec ratings to determine the
>performance of the machine.   The truth is that right now , in their
>current implementation, quad or six cpu ppro's waste plenty of cycles
>just talking to each other, so you can't just *add* the specmarks to
>each other. The alphas have 128 and 256 bit wide memory access while the
>ppro has only 64bits.  And to top it off most alpha's can support up to
>8megs of secondary cache.( yes I know the binaries are larger, but 8megs
>is much better than 256k.)   There are many things that figure into a
>working benchmark.  The best thing is to test your application on the
>machine you want.   If your application wasn't multithreaded, you would
>see *less* performance on the quad ppro than the alpha.
>
>mike

Maybe so, but don't you think 28g is too much for a workstation. And I
never stated that a SMP PPro would kick Alpha's power. But there is a
price/performance curve, and I feel that the Alpha does not have a
good bang for the buck. I mean if you want power, try multiple RISCs.
But the Alpha is not an SMP applied chip (that doesn't mean it could
be), and it is easier to use PPros because of the standardized
architecture, and not to mention the huge base of software.
Furthermore, yes, the SMP PPro would waste cycles, but it would
definetly match the Alpha on 'most' apps. Look at some workstation
performance comparisions and you'll see that a dual PPro is very
comparable to a 366 or 400 Alpha, and in my humble opinion, a quad
ought to be equal if not faster that a 500 Alpha, of course using the
same disk subsystem. And the recently introduced Diamond 4000 would
wreak havoc on the Alpha's graphics card (check original poster- he
had a zd something with glint and 8vram /32 dram on it). So for a
third of the price, a system comparable to the Alpha would be built
and would perform close to it.



