> 
> All that I was saying was for ten grand, I could build a machine that
> would be comparable to that 28 grand Alpha. Even though the Alpha has a
> better FPU unit, a dual PPro would be close to that power, and a quad
> ought to surpass it.

You shouldn't rely so heavily on spec ratings to determine the
performance of the machine.   The truth is that right now , in their
current implementation, quad or six cpu ppro's waste plenty of cycles
just talking to each other, so you can't just *add* the specmarks to
each other. The alphas have 128 and 256 bit wide memory access while the
ppro has only 64bits.  And to top it off most alpha's can support up to
8megs of secondary cache.( yes I know the binaries are larger, but 8megs
is much better than 256k.)   There are many things that figure into a
working benchmark.  The best thing is to test your application on the
machine you want.   If your application wasn't multithreaded, you would
see *less* performance on the quad ppro than the alpha.

mike



