Jeffrey Potoff wrote:
> 
> Cheap Alphas are great, but there's one thing that really
> bothers me.  The spec numbers they post for those configurations
> are completely bogus.

Best laugh I've had in a while!

Consider how bogus the Intel numbers most people are throwing around:

  - Typical numbers were gathered using a motherboard that almost
    no Intel users use (e.g. Alder, only used by some high-end HP boxes
    or via when obtained via special order from Intel)

  - Typical numbers were gathered using an operating system that almost
    no Intel users use (the Intel port of System V)

  - Typical numbers were gathered using a compiler that almost no Intel 
    users use (the Intel reference compilers)

At least Digital's numbers are gathered using the exact same systems
you can order from Digital, using the same operating system and
compilers (and the operating system was compiled using those
compilers!).  Granted, some of the compilation flags are funky, but
that is the typical case for all SPEC submissions.

I'd love to see the SPEC results for an off the shelf mail-order
PC using Microsoft C and Fortran when running a Microsoft operating
system.

