In article <5jc3u6$50i$1@madrid.visi.net>, downlink@visi.net (Jason Witty) writes:

:I recently aquired a VAXserver 3300 and would like to know what type of 
:terminal I need to use in order to access it.  What I need to know is 
:what VT#, and whether or not the terminal has to be made by DEC.  This is 
:my first VAX experience, so I am really looking forward to it.  Thanks 
:very much.

   Most any VT series terminal, from the ancient VT52 series onward,
   can be used.  For reasons ranging from physical size and available
   features to relative age, I would look for a VT3xx, VT4xx, or later.

   PC-based terminal emulators and third-party ANSI-standard serial
   terminals can also be used, but if you're asking this question, I'd
   start with a DIGITAL terminal. 

   The DECconnect (MMJ) pinouts and adapters are listed in the OpenVMS
   Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) monthly posting.  (If you do not
   have a copy of the FAQ, get it -- it's quite useful...  Copies are
   available at various sites including http://www.openvms.digital.com.)

   Various operating systems can be used; I'd expect this system would
   be using OpenVMS,  (Also see comp.os.vms.)

