In article <1997Apr18.225544.1@cpg.mcw.edu>, 
brodie@cpg.mcw.edu (Kent C Brodie) writes:

> With all the talk about new technology...  pentium desktop stations,
> Alphaservers running windows/NT, dialup-tcpip (PPP/etc), client-server,  and
> the rest--  I'd like to remind folks that the KISS rule should still apply.  
> That is, NEW technology isn't necessarily better.

> For text-display purposes, I still have yet to find ANY display device that
> has such bright and clear text as my might VT.

One of my customers has an application that requires displaying
132-character lines of text.   We found that the best terminal
for 132-character mode was made by Microterm.   They used a
very dense  matrix that made it look as good (or better) than
other vendor's 80-column mode (hello, DEC).

Unfortunately, they are no longer in business and my customer has
bought all the remaining terminals that they can find (and would
like more).   So, what do they do?   DEC's terminals have a 132-column
mode that is so crisp and sharp that you can see every pixel.   That
is the problem.   Just try looking at it all day long.   DEC's
terminals just won't hack it in 132-column mode like Microterm's can.

The tidal wave in the industry is towards computers on the desktop, so
we decided that we must change or be swept away.   Since the application
is VMS-based we would have liked to just put a Multia-class machine
on every desktop.   However, DEC does not want VMS on the desktop,
so we must port the application to something like WNT.   This will
cost us a *LOT*.   Thank you, DEC.   NOT!

While the porting is going on, we still need to get terminals for
new users (and to replace dead terminals).   Since available terminals
have poor 132-column text, and because we are moving the application
to WNT, we would like to buy PCs and use them as terminal emulators
until the port is completed.

Now, try to find a PC video card that offers decent 132-column text
mode.   I cannot.   Also, monochrome monitors are almost impossible
to find these days, and they are essential if the text is to be clear
enough for all-day use.

All this grief, just because we can no longer buy dumb terminals
with decent 132-column mode.   Give me KISS any day.

