In article <5iegad$fqk$1@news.ccit.arizona.edu>, jshin@aruba.u.arizona.edu (Jae H Shin) writes:
> dear netters,
> 
> according to a document about DECstation 5000/240(DEC order# EC-N0194-51)
> serial ports on DS5000/240 supports upto 208.4k baud, which is over 5 times
> faster than that of 5000/133(upto 38.4k baud).
> anybody can confirm that fact?

I can confirm that it does *not* work.

The 5000/240s I've seen use Zilog SCC (8530) chips, the same chips
used in Macintoshes for LocalTalk.  The hardware is capable at
running at up to 230.4 kbps *synchronous*.   

But in asynch mode, the SCC chip only works at speeds up to 57.6k. 

AFAIK, you can program the chip to run faster than that; but in asynch mode
it just doesn't get the input characters correctly.  The external
clock that the motherboard feeds to the SCC chip is too slow to let
the chip sample the RS-232 bits accurately at higher speeds. The
result is similar to line noise; lots and lots of characters with the
top bit set, not very useful.
Corrections are welcomed :).

NetBSD/pmax lets you set speeds up to 115.2; but they've never worked
for me.  Your mileage may vary. I have no idea what the Ultrix driver
supports.

