In article <1997Apr18.225544.1@cpg.mcw.edu>, brodie@cpg.mcw.edu (Kent C Brodie) writes:
> With all the talk about new technology...  pentium desktop stations,
> Alphaservers running windows/NT, dialup-tcpip (PPP/etc), client-server,  and
> the rest--  I'd like to remind folks that the KISS rule should still apply.  
> That is, NEW technology isn't necessarily better.      The following BRIEF
> story drives this point home:
> 
> -----
> 
> The other night, I was on the telephone with a support person in Boston
> (Intersystems).   It was the wee hours of the night, and I was getting
> assistance in resolving a critical problem.    The support programmer
> told me that after he dialed into my system, he'd poke around and then
> call me back right away.    He closed the conversation stating that it would
> "be about 20 minutes" before he could dial in and call me back.
> 
> When I queried about the length of time, his response was that most of that
> time was necessary for his system to "boot up and get things configured to
> dial in". 
> 
> Meanwhile, my "desktop unit" at home is nothing more than a simple VT100
> (yes, a real VT100..  and it doesn't have any fish), coupled with a simple
> 19.2 multitech modem.     The total time for me to be logged in at the DCL
> prompt on my cluster... INCLUDING power-up time:    31 seconds.
> 
> There's something to be said for simple technology.   And you know what?
> For text-display purposes, I still have yet to find ANY display device that
> has such bright and clear text as my might VT.

Once you have logged into the remote system, can you download log files to your
vt100 with kermit and look at them offline?  Can you upload diagnostic tools
and other files?   If the target site is on the internet, can you ftp back to
yourself to move files back and forth?   Can you use telnet instead of dialing
in if the target site has this capability?  Can you capture your terminal
session in any way (you don't mention having a printer with your vt100)?  Can
you roll your window back to view stuff that has scrolled off the screen?   
Can you cut and paste from your session window or from other windows into your
command line?  I can do all of this when I am dialed in.

Yes, the dumb terminal is viewable, yes, you can connect fast, but you are also
much more limited in what you can do.  I will continue using my workstation for
dialing into remote sites, thank you.  I don't want to be crippled while I am
dialed in, and want to make the best use of the connect time, which is almost
always long distance for me.

The dumb terminal belongs on the trash heap of history, rubbing shoulders with
the buggy whip, the 8-track tape, and leisure suits.

I *do* have a vt220 acting as console terminal on a microvax.  This is probably
the one use they are suited for, as long as you don't mind the messages
scrolling off to be lost forever (I don't have a printer connected to mine
either).  I leave the vt220 turned off unless I am booting, because all
messages that show up there also show up in the console window on the vrc21,
where they are scrollable.

Wayne
