In article <01bc4456$e0a4bc60$5f0520ce@coolio-s-server>, "Dennis Moran" <coolio@coolio9.com> wrote:

>No way. The best way I can think of this is consoles and console emulators.
>Running a game for a SNES is slower on a 100MHz Pentium than the slow 8MHz
>or so processor in the SNES, because it is emulated by software on the
>Pentium system.

Not a valid comparison, the CPU emulation on (illegal) SNES emulators takes 
very little host CPU compared with emulating the array of custom hardware in 
the system.

Compare something like UAE (Amiga emulator) with ARDI's Executor Mac emulator. 
Both similar Motorola 68K CPU's being emulated, but UAE is significantly 
slower due to the array of Amiga hardware that must also be emulated even 
though the actual CPU in the Amiga is many times slower than a Mac. 
(Forgetting about the fact that ARDI's 68K emulation is faster than UAE's to 
start with).

Besides, FX!32's a (very clever) compiler for x86 instructions and API 
calls and not an emulator anyway. You can't get an emulator to optimise it's 
translations in the background while not running the emulated app.

