Hello everybody,

the following article may partly sound a bit rude, but this is written by
someone who is currently extremely frustrated with his DEC machine...

I have an AlphaStation 500/266, 2 RZ28 disks, internal RRD45 CD-ROM and an
additional Tandberg TDC3660 streamer externally attached.  When I got this
machine about a year ago, it had Digital Unix 3.2D-1 installed.  Trouble with
the streamer started upon the first boot: the SCSI driver (ISP1020) wrote 7
messages about timeouts for LUNs 1-7 of the streamer's ID and needed about
a quarter of an hour to recover from them until booting continued.  I did
not have such effects with the DEC3000/300X I used before, but I thought
"Well, you don't reboot every day" and smiled.  I also smiled about the
fact that xmcd (a popular Audio-CD-player software) needed a few minutes to
log in a new CD.

Two weeks ago, we made an update to DU 4.0B .  I was told that DU4.0 contained
better support for non-DEC SCSI devices, and I was hoping to get rid of these
annoying side-effects.  The result was:

The DU 4.0D ISP driver does not only print timeout messages for the SCSI
streamer, it dies with a kernel panic after the first message!  I have to boot
with the streamer turned off to get my system up!  xmcd's behaviour has also
become worse: If I start xmcd and there is currently no CD in the drive, the
drive becomes completely "locked": any further access, regardless if I want to
mount a data CDROM or play an Audio CD returns a "no such device" message though
the drive is still there and a CD is inserted!  I have to reboot the machine to 
make the CD-ROM accessible again!

The field service told us that there is a console command 'isp1020_edit' that
allows to make some NVRAM settings.  Well...it prints a hexdump of  128 bytes
and lets you modify individual bytes, but no explanation about their meaning!

I always had a high opinion about the quality of DEC hardware, but I'm slowly
about to loose it.  Does anyone know a way to modify the ISP driver's be-
haviour?

P.S. Please omit statements like "Your streamer/CD player software is
     incompatible" - They work flawlessly on about half a dozen of other
     systems, and I won't accept such answers.
     

