Oh, lighten up and consider an alternative parse...  for "it runs on",
try reading "each runs on"; the claim that's being made here is that

	linux uses generally the same basic kernel for all
	the different platforms it runs on

and

	WindowsNT uses generally the same basic kernel for all
        the different platforms it runs on

but not that Linux and WinNT are the same kernel.  I agree with this
claim myself.  About 2/3rds of a kernel is hardware independant
(things like filesystems, networking, the scheduler).  Stuff that's
different between platforms tends mostly to fall into two categories:

  a) devices, dma and interrupts
  b) memory management and protection

I might add that although device drivers vary greatly within an OS
class depending on platform, device drivers for a given platform tend
to be pretty similar between OS's - I mean, how many ways are there to
write (for example) a serial port driver for given chipset after all?

There's also been a lot of experience gained in abstracting out the
mem management issues as well; as an example the Mach code and ideas
have found their way into a lot of places (and then again there's the
NT "HAL" layer - same idea).

regards,
