From: Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>
Subject: Re: enorex vs dec
Date: 1997/04/16
Message-ID: <qxpohbel9in.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 235334312
References: <3354EFD2.41C67EA6@hustat.harvard.edu>
Organization: University of Colorado
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.alpha



The PC164 uses a DRAM memory subsystem. The PWS 433 & 500 use
Synchronous DRAM. In my configuration (PWS 433a vs. 500Mhz PC164), I
see about 70% higher memory bandwidth from the PWS 433. However, I
didn't order a cache for the PWS, and eliminating cache typically
improves bandwidth (but hits mean latency). I was running Linux on the
PWS and Unix on the PC164, but ran the same binary on both.

If you run big "out-of-cache" jobs, the PWS is a good machine. If you
need a bigger cache, the PC164 works well.


-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



