I am one who will admit if I made a mistake, and I did. I checked out
the price, and am astounded by it. The offer was not bad, it is quite
nice considering the retail price, but even so, looking at
workstations now, this would not be a good price/performance unit. A
quad pentium pro (ALR or HP) and a diamond FireGL 4000 series 3D card
would 'probably' outperform that maching for around half the price.
And if you are really on a tight budget, do what I did:
Micron Pro2 (2 pentium pro 200's)		5000
	(level B, SCSI config)
upgrade to decent 21"           		750
(I have 64) go for 128:			500
Diamond 4000:				3000

I paid only 5000 for a dual pentium pro, but by adding an extra four
thousand, you can have a kick ass machine for around 1/3 the alpha's
price. So it's not as fast on floating point, but NT and backward
compatibility make up for that. And the Diamond 4000 would be faster
than the Glint 500TX powered Powerstorm. So overall, around ten
thousand, you would have one of the best graphics (3D, 2D)
workstations, approaching a cheap SGI, and a crazy crack-powered PC.

Please understand, I'm not saying that the Alpha workstation is bad,
but it is not comparable to the current crop of workstations. It is
not that a 500mhz Alpha is not fast enough, quite opposite, but the
bang for the buck is not on the Alpha's side.

I have a dec multia and I like it, but even so, the Alpha if
multiprocessor would be the killer combo. DEC priced its 500 Alpha too
high for it to be comparable to a dual pentium pro.

And as I said above, for 28 grand, I would go for a quad pentium pro
or even one of the newer 6 pentium pro computers. You are approaching
800 mips on a dual, 1500 mips (1.5 bips) on a quad, and well above 2
bips on a six, close to 3 on an eight. A 500mhz alpha does (in
reality, not on paper) a bip and change. A quad would be a better
deal, and a eight pentium pro (to be released from ALR) would destroy
it. After all NT supports 8 processors most, and that would be the
best bang.

