Dan Stromberg wrote:

<snip>

> 1) Is there a software RAID implementation for Sun, SGI or DEC that works
>    reliabily, and Predictably?

For our Suns we use Online Disksuite which seems to work fine.

> 2) We want the ability to "grow filesystems", whether we go with hardware
>    or software RAID.  Are there any (hardware RAID)+OS combinations that allow
>    growing filesystems without some sort of software volume management?

I'm certainly not an expert but, looking at how various RAID
implementations work, I would have thought that this sort of facility
would be very unlikely. Does anyone know for sure?

> 4) If hardware RAID would require software volume management to grow
>    filesystems, would we be better off just going with software RAID anyway?

See above. IMHO the main benefit of hardware RAID is that the additional
overhead is removed from the file server. Something like RAID 5 is quite
resource intensive, particularly for writing, because of the way the
parity data is handled. This not only means more CPU is used but the i/o
to the disks can be quite high. A hardware RAID appears like a big disk
to the host so the amount of CPU loading and i/o to the RAID should be
no greater than for a normal disk. All the extra processing and i/o is
local to the RAID, between its controller and the disks.

> 5) What vendor(s) would you recommend for your preferred RAID solution?

We use Sun's ODS and Sparc E3000s (some of which have SPARCstorage
Arrays) for a software/hardware solution as well as EuroLogic el-RAID
hardware RAIDs on various platforms (Suns, HPs and NetWare PC file
servers). They seem to work fine although I'm sure other RAIDs are just
as good.

