In article <E8Myyo.5tE@novice.uwaterloo.ca>, dfevans@bbcr.uwaterloo.ca (David
Evans) writes:
>In article <335209BE.74589321@usdesign.com>,
>Charles McCrobie  <mccrobie@usdesign.com> wrote:
>>
>>We have a Q-BUS card that I'm not sure what it does:
>>
>>        M7551-CA   memory module?  What machine does this work in?

>  According to http://www.neurotica.com/dec-info/, this is a 1-Mbyte parity
>MOS
>memory (11/73, 11/53+, 11/53).  Looks like it's PDP 11-only.

It will NOT work for a uVAX II.  Sorry.

>
>>The RX50 won't show up at SYSGEN AUTOCONFIG ALL.  In fact, at the
>>sleeping
>>sargent, B DUB0 and B DUB1 produces "DEVOFFLINE".  The controller does
>>show up
>>as PUB0.  I believe the RX50 unit itself is broken.  Power to the unit
>>is good.

Removable media drives usually report to VMS that the device is offline if
there is no media mounted when an access is attempted.  That doen't mean the
unit really works, just something to check up on and note.

>  Are there any other disks attached to the controller (I assume a RQDX3)?
>Sometimes a dead disk can hose other devices that are attached.

This is true also.

>>It appears that only two MS630-BB memory modules may be installed.  Am I
>>correct is assuming the maximum memory for this machine is 8 (9) MB?
>
>  The maximum is actually 16 MB; there exist 8 MB cards, although I don't
>remember the two-letter combination after MS630 for them.

The uVAX II is only capable of addressing 16 Mb of memory (if I remember this
correctly).  That's why when two 8 Mb boards are present, the one Mb of
memory on the CPU board is disabled (you get 16, not 17Mb) but if you have
only one board in, you get 9 instead of 8Mb.

>>I
>>have
>>seen advertisements for 16 MB Q-BUS memory cards.  Will these work in
>>the
>>MicroVAX II and is it possible to install 3 such cards (in slots 2,3,
>>and 4)
>>for a total of 3*16 + 1 or 49 MB?

Will they work?  Probably not.  They would have to have been specifically
designed for the uVAX II.  Can you put multiple cards in the system?  Yes, 
but even if they were designed for the uVAX II, you would only be able to 
access 16Mb of memory, no matter how much extra you put in.

>>I've seen DEQNA boards for this machine.  Are there any "got-chas" with
>>the
>>DEQNA and if so, what other Ethernet boards are available?

DEC tried to get all the DEQNAs out of circulation because of a problem with
DECnet I believe.  This was some time ago, but they were offering upgrades
to a DEQNA+ board, which had the "fix".  I would try to get a DELQA, a DESQA
or some other newer Ethernet board/LAN adaptor.

>>Is it possible to get a graphics board for this machine?  I have NOT
>>seen a
>>graphics card advertised for this machine.  What would be the cost?
>
>  Yes.  You would want the board set from a VAXstation II/GPX--the QDSS as I
>recall.  The boards are <shuffle shuffle> M7169 VCB02 Base Module and M7168
>VCB02 4-plane module.  This will get you 16 colours; you can stick in another
>M7168 and get 256.

I wouldn't count on this being very fast.

>>Would such graphics boards be supported under DEC Windows Motif 1.2-4
>
>  Dunno.

I don't know either.  You'll have to give it a try.

>>Oh, we have OpenVMS 7.1 running on the machine now.  Installed and
>>booted without a problem!

Not too surprising.  I would expect it to work under 7.1 since the
documentation says it should.  As far as the rest goes, uVAX II parts are
now so cheap, you should have no trouble finding and installing anything
you want at little cost.  It was a VERY popular machine, so there should be
LOTS of used stuff out there.

