Peter Lasky wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> I have one of these neat little pizza-box DEC 433W PCs.  I had run if for
> some time booting off a floppy and loading files across a TR network.
> I now want to add a hard drive and noted that it takes a 2.5" internal.
> I thought this was standard IDE, but the connector is pretty strange.
> Does anyone have any info on what type of drive or cable this uses?
> I would like to have a small bootable internal drive, and can use either
> the network or an external SCSI for more intensve work.
> Please e-mail me with any info - I don't have the manuals :-(
> 
> Thanks,
> peter@cumsl.ctr.columbia.edu

There were DECpc 433 Workstation (433W) configurations where the
internal disketter drive was swapped for a small internal IDE 
hard drive.

The DECpc 433 has an on-board equivalent of the ISA Adaptec 
1510/1520 SCSI adapter with the small 50-pin "Honda" connector
for external devices (such as the DECpc 433W SCSI expansion box,
which is more or less the same thing as for the MIPS-based
DECstation boxes of the same - circa '91 - era).

In any case, anything that'll run a 486 with Adaptec AHA-1510/1520
and plain VGA should work on the box. If it supports the Texas
Instruments' TIGA then you'll get the 1024x768 resolution support
(or with IBM 8514/A API drivers - booting through DOS - you can
get such software to work; the TIGA isn't fully register compatible
with the 8514/A).

Technically both the video and IDE are on standard buses, but the
connectors aren't.

regards,
 ...petri.backstrom@icl.fi
    ICL Data Oy
    Finland

P.S. The integrated Token Ring is an Olicom variant, and in
     the Ethernet version the chip is AMD LANCE based and
     works with DEC EtherWORKS/LC (DE100) drivers.

N.B. Try http://www.windows.digital.com/ for whatever drivers & 
     other stuff that might still be available for this box.



