Subject: Re: *Challenge!* Can you hack OPEN VMS?
Date: 1997/04/15
Message-ID: <5j0jfm$jkh$1@mrnews.mro.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 235079215
Distribution: world
References: <33536971.1869@tnm.co.uk> <5j0320$o8g@transfer.stratus.com>
Organization: I speak ONLY for myself. Nobody else, no organization.
Reply-To: Everhart@Arisia.GCE.Com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



Details of how to get into a VMS system where one has physical access
to the machine but forgotten passwords are published in the OVMS
security guide and are I think in the FAQ.

One simple thing to try is boot interactively, e.g.
>>>b -fl 0,1

then to the sysboot prompt

SET UAFALTERNATE 1
SET WINDOW_SYSTEM 0
SET WRITESYSPARAM 0
CONT

Then log into the console when it finishes boot...any password will
do, log in as SYSTEM.

If there IS a uafalternate.dat you'll have to drop back and set the
startup to OPA0: which requires more care.

When logged in as system, you can assign the real uaf file to uafalternate
and reset passwords...

Then shut down & reboot. The "writesyspar" trick will mean the parameters
don't need to be undone for next time.

This is handy where you get a used machine, not knowing passwords.

For unix, btw, there is also a drill that has to do with booting
single user, then remounting the system partition with some switch
like -u (anyone recall detail?) to permit writing, then resetting
the /etc/passwd file...

(and be sure to sync).

Anyone know if there's a drill for breaking into an NT system with a
forgotten admin password?



