>Linux already runs on Alpha, x86 and ARM.

On some Alphas. It doesn't run on the Turbochannel ones.

And evidently the ARM support isn't working well enough to be
integrated into the standard Linux distribution. I just downloaded
the latest kernel I could find (2.1.34) and there's no sign of ARM
support there at all.

>A port to VAX is underway.

But not to the stage where it can even boot yet. As the porter says
in his sigfile: `no progress to report.' NetBSD has been running
stably on some VAXen for over a year now.

And let's not forget the MIPS-based DECStation port of Linux, which
last I heard had been holding steady for a year or so at printing
the copyright and bombing. NetBSD's been running stably on that
platform for over a year now, too.

As well, Linux has yet to even start on an I/O model for device
drivers which will allow them to be bus-independent. Under NetBSD,
for example, the exact same driver runs all 3COM cards, from the
3c509 to the 3c905, regardless of what bus and what machine they're
running on. (Each bus variant has a separate bit of code to set up
the I/O system, of course.) Have you tried to use a 3c590 or 3c900
in an Alpha? Does it work?

