shea@red.seas.upenn.edu (Dan Shea) writes:

> I saw this on http://www.microway.com/mbpx.html

>> Digital has made a marketing decision to charge more for Alpha UNIX
>> processors. If you purchase an Alpha designated for NT and try to load
>> UNIX it will not  run. 

Unix == Digital Unix != Linux

>> Internally the processor is different. 

Last time I looked processors (a.k.a. CPUs) do come in different
flavours, they all run NT, and they all run D.U. AFAIK.

>> Digital charges us for the SRM console plus license fees to run
>> Digital UNIX. 

> Is this true?

Probably.

> If so will linux work on the "NT processor" or do I need to shell out
> for a Digital Unix box (over 3k more easily) just to run Linux?

The difference between a D.U. and an NT box is the console, called ARC
for NT (ugly blue screen with big font, and menu selections) and SRM for
Unix (black screen, tiny font, command line interface).  Some boxes come
with both, try selecting ``boot other operating system'' or some such
within ARC, or just typing ``arc'' in SRM to switch. 

Linux will boot happily from either one, I think, but is usually booted
from ARC (originally you needed SRM).

There may be other issues, like memory subsystem and such that needs
support, for instance, TurboChannel architectures are not supported
under neither NT nor Linux, and rumours would have it that some new
designs won't be supported under Digital Unix.

~kzm
