In article <335209BE.74589321@usdesign.com>,
Charles McCrobie <mccrobie@usdesign.com> wrote:
> We have a Micro VAX II, CPU KA630-A.V1.3 Q-BUS machine.  I've looked at
> Digital's WEB pages and Alta-vista and found some suppliers of Q-BUS
> cards, but no "definitive" information as to the possible configurations
> of memory,
> ethernet, and graphics cards for Q-BUS machines.

> It appears that only two MS630-BB memory modules may be installed.  Am I
> correct is assuming the maximum memory for this machine is 8 (9) MB?  I
> have
> seen advertisements for 16 MB Q-BUS memory cards.  Will these work in
> the
> MicroVAX II and is it possible to install 3 such cards (in slots 2,3,
> and 4)
> for a total of 3*16 + 1 or 49 MB?

No.  The largest memory board that will work with the MVII is 8MB, and you
can put only 2 memory boards in one.  Additionally there is a processor
limit of 16MB, since with 2 8MB boards you get only 16MB, not 17MB (there is
1MB on the CPU card)  With 2 4MB cards you get 9MB.

> I've seen DEQNA boards for this machine.  Are there any "got-chas" with
> the
> DEQNA and if so, what other Ethernet boards are available?

The DEQNA is troublesome on busy ethernets and will "go to sleep".  The
VMS driver detects this and wakes it.  However, it is troublesome enough
that VMS desupported it (at least for clusters) in favor of the DELQA.
If you get a DELQA (or even better, a DELQA-YM) use it instead.
Certain revs of the DEQNA are more problematic than others.

> Is it possible to get a graphics board for this machine?  I have NOT
> seen a
> graphics card advertised for this machine.  What would be the cost?
> Would such graphics boards be supported under DEC Windows Motif 1.2-4
> (if not, what software will support graphics boards for the machine?) 
> What
> type of monitor is required for above graphics boards?

There is the QDSS (2 or 3 board set for 4 or 8 planes color) it uses the VR290
monitor (among others)  If you are into self-abuse there is the QVSS 1 (!)
plane black and white setup (that's right, boys and girls, when I said black
and white I meant it. No shades of gray!)  I am not sure whether the latest
DECwindows will run on the QDSS, I know earlier versions did.  Think the QVSS
is VWS (old windowing system) only.

> Oh, we have OpenVMS 7.1 running on the machine now.  Installed and
> booted without a problem!

VMS is pretty good at supporting old stuff as long as there is enough memory
and disk space available for it.

-Mike



