In article <dneel.76.00175CED@epix.net>, dneel@epix.net (Dave Neel) writes:
:First question: How does one run with a terminal as the console? There is a 
:dip switch by the serial port, but I don't know which position is which. Also, 
:what are the comm settings needed on the terminal. 

   S3 <up> selects the alternate console, while S3 <down> selects the
   graphics console.  Connect the serial terminal to the printer port,
   select S3 <up> (don't use a pencil to do this, as graphite conducts
   electricity and can potentially damage a slide switch), and set the
   terminal for 9600 baud.  Press <BREAK> on the terminal a few times,
   and you should see the >>> prompt from the console.

:I have a VR241 monitor but I can't seem to get this to work. Is this the
:right one anyway?

   Nope, the VR241 was the monitor used for the VR241 color terminal.

   You will want a VR260, VR290, or compatible.  1024 x 864 x 60 Hz,
   synch-on-green (GPX), or the (optional) 1280 x 1024 x 66 Hz SPX
   graphics.  Most of these systems have the GPX graphics controller.

:Next, all I can tell is when the box is powered up, it is going through some 
:test because there is activity on the little LED bank on the back. The drive 
:doesn't spin up,but there is activity on the ethernet. How does one get the 
:disk to spin up for a local boot?

   OpenVMS spins up the drive when it is mounted.  (The PC model that
   spins-up the drives on power-up tends to require, uh, rather larger
   power supplies in larger configuratations.)

   Do not exceed a system disk maximum capacity of 1.073GB with any
   OpenVMS release used in any member of the VAXstation 3100 series.
   (Data disks can be up to 8.5 GB on pre-V6.0, and 1 TB on V6.0 and
   later releases.)

