>Unix, since I thought that the GEM engine was used for
>both Unix and NT VC++?

Yes, it's the same back-end. The problem is that on Unix, we use the DEC
C++ 
front-end, which of course Digital controls. On NT, we use the Microsoft
VC++ 
front-end. The Microsoft front-end is not specifically tuned for
Digital, and 
doesn't tell us as much information as the Unix front-end does. I think
that's 
mostly historical, because Microsoft has had dumb compilers. PC users 
generally are more concerned about compile speed than code quality, and
VC++ 
reflects that. Unix users are just the opposite, so DEC C++ conveys a
richer 
intermediate language than VC++ does.

We are working much more closely with Microsoft these days, and both
companies 
are interested in improving VC++, so it should get better quickly.
------------------------------------------------------------------



