"Dennis" == Dennis O'Connor <dmoc@primenet.com> writes:
 >  Paul DeMone wrote in article <33503885.CAF@EasyInternet.net>...
 >> 1) Process frequency yield curve
 >> 
 >> The distribution of maximum operating clock frequency of CMOS
 >> logic chips, including CMOS processors, fabricated in large
 >> volumes in a given process generally give a nice bell-curve or
 >> normal distribution.

 > I do not think that is true.  If the yielded frequency followed a
 > normal distribution, there would be some parts that ran correctly
 > but very slowly, and others that ran correctly at very high speeds,
 > over the rated operating conditions. I do not believe this is true.

	No, I do believe that Paul is correct in stating that there is
   a great deal of variation over a process.  Why else do you think 
   you have so many speed grades of a processor when they are made on 
   the same process. They are all ( in most cases ) from the same process
   but are then binned into the speed at which they work correctly.
   
        Additionally almost every semi-conductor house simulates circuits
   for a weak, nominal and a strong process ( at the very least ).  A weak
   process may be at one extreme 3 sigma point of the curve, and a strong at
   the other extreme.  This is to ensure that the circuit is functionally 
   correct over a wide process variation range.

 > Proccess variations are tightly controlled : parameters such as
 > line widths and effective channel length and dopant concentrations
 > and oxide thickness do not vary a lot in functional die, at least
 > not at Intel, as near as I know.  And physics dictates that if
 > these parameters are confined to a range, so is the speed at which
 > the circuits can switch.

	While process variations are tightly controlled it is difficult to
   achieve precise channel length. e.g. Lateral diffusion variations will cause
   channel length to vary.  While it is true that the distribution need not
   be normal, it is often approximated to  one ( because there are
   a lot of independent factors which cause the curve to be gaussian - since
   you are a statistician -  Central Limit Theorem ). Note that the original
   poster is NOT just talking  about variations across a single die, or a wafer, 
   BUT also across a batch of wafers. 

 > I'm not saying there  isnt a distribution in the yielded frequency
 > of parts; I'm saying it is not a normal distribution.

   True, however this doesn't change the fact that process variations are
   probably one of the most  significant factors for circuit speed 
   varying on the same process.

 > Off course, this is just my dilletennte's opinion. I'm not a fab
 > engineer at Intel.  But I do have training in stastics: the normal
 > distribution is not the only one possible. -- Dennis O'Connor
	
 > dmoc@primenet.com Intel employee and stockholder. Not speaking for
 > anyone else.

