> much as possible.  The core routines of the QCD benchmark are
> latency
> rather than bandwith limited on many machines, so you can speed it
> up
> tremendously by pipelining and/or prefetching.

This is correct.... I was wondering about how to speed things up even
more.
When I made the routines on the CM5 (in assembly level CDPEAC),
I stored the data in such a way as to optimize for speed on the vector
units.
So  the SU(3) matrices and vectors (the basic "objects" in the QCD code)

were not stored in one place. Do you know what I mean? I "vectorized
over matrix multiplications", if you understand what I am saying. Rob
Harley
increased the speed of the QCD routines quite a lot, but he was still
constrained by the basic structure I imposed when I wrote this first
portable version of the code. I was thinking that perhaps storing the
matrices
and vectors differently as I did on the CM5, it could be possible to
efficiently "strip-mine" the data a la CM5. It may be that there are
not enough registers to do this though. I used to use all 128 registers
on
the CM5 in my CDPEAC routines!! There certainly are not enough
"registers"
on Intel based computers. Does anyone know if the Intel fpu will ever
progress beyond the Mickey Mouse 7-level stack disaster?

These changes are not related to what I have been working on recently,
BTW. My recent thrust has been to reduce the need to call the data
shifting routines nearly as often.

Oh, one other thing. The routines in this code can be made very
efficient
through the use of combined mult-add instructions. Unfortunately, I
understand that the Alpha does not have such a thing... which is a pity.

Is this true?
A combined load-mult-add instruction would be perfect, of course.

cheers

Henley




