Dave Neel wrote:
 
> First question: How does one run with a terminal as the console? There is a
> dip switch by the serial port, but I don't know which position is which. Also,
> what are the comm settings needed on the terminal.

The "alternate console switch" needs to be flipped UP; this switches
from
monitor to console mode. This switch is the one located next to the
Right/Left
arrow diagram port on back of the VAXstation 3100.

The cable to the monitor must be connected to the port that has the
printer
diagram on it.

The basic terminal settings are 9600 baud, xon/xoff flow control, 8
bits,
no parity, 1 stop bit. Those are what we're using on our dumb terminals 
we're using for console devices.
 
> I have a VR241 monitor but I can't seem to get this to work. Is this the right
> one anyway?

I can't help you there.  We've used VT320's, VT420's, and VT520's. I
don't know
what a VR241 is. If it's a dumb terminal, it probably should work.

> Next, all I can tell is when the box is powered up, it is going through some
> test because there is activity on the little LED bank on the back. The drive
> doesn't spin up,but there is activity on the ethernet. How does one get the
> disk to spin up for a local boot?

If (when) you get the console to work, you should be able to see the box
go through its tests. You don't say what kind of VAXstation 3100 it is.
We have model 38's and model 76's -- they come up and start the backward
countdown (in hex, from F...E...D... and so on) as they do the testing
of 
the various devices, memory, etc. Eventually, you'll get the prompt >>>
from where you can see what devices are available, what the boot device
is set to, etc.

Good luck.

Robin E. Shahan
resh@chevron.com
My opinions, not Chevron's.



