 Paul DeMone wrote in article <33503885.CAF@EasyInternet.net>...
>1) Process frequency yield curve
>
>   The distribution of maximum operating clock frequency of CMOS
>   logic chips, including CMOS processors, fabricated in large
>   volumes in a given process generally give a nice bell-curve
>   or normal distribution.

I do not think that is true.  If the yielded frequency
followed a normal distribution, there would be some parts
that ran correctly but very slowly, and others that ran
correctly at very high speeds, over the rated operating
conditions. I do not believe this
 is true.  Proccess
variations are tightly controlled : parameters such
as line widths and effective channel length and
dopant concentrations and oxide thickness do not 
vary a lot in functional die, at least not at Intel, as
near as I know.  And physics dictates that if these
 parameters
 are confined to a range, so is the speed 
at which
 the circuits can switch.

I'm not saying there  isnt a distribution in the
yielded frequency of parts; I'm saying it is not
a normal distribution.  

Off course, this is just my dilletennte's opinion.
I'm not a fab engineer at Intel.  But I do have
training in stastics: the normal distribution
is not the only one possible.
--
