motherboard produced by DEC Semiconductor. It uses 72-pin parity
SIMMs. The kicker is that because it uses a 128 or 256-bit wide data
bus, the SIMMs have to be banked in either four or eight of the same
size. The new motherboard coming out this summer, which will have
support for 600Mhz part and will have 2MB of L3 cache, will use SDRAM,
168-pin DIMMs. These are also standard parts.


