Okay, permit me to delve into this a little more.

Again, don't confuse "emulation" with "translation" (I know, I'm
preaching to the choir, but there's an audience here).  Sure, emulating
a SNES is slow on a Pentium, but if you could translate that 8MHz code
into native x86 instructions with an optimizing compiler (a la RISC, not
CISC) or a dedicated assembler team, I'll bet you'd get better
performance on the rePentium.

My comment was more directed towards the fact that hardware and
microcode designers have to consider all possible scenarios of flow
through their pipes and registers.  The hardware has to be built to
handle all possible circumstances -- optimizing it for one particular
function or module is expensive in terms of real estate (take your
choice -- more hardware to implement a function, or larger ROM for
specialized firmware).  This is the bloat found in CISC architectures.

Contrast that with translating the code to a streamlined RISC
environment where the "compiler" can optimize the software on a module
by module, thread by thread, stream by stream.  Sure, it may take up
more disk space and more RAM to store and load it and more cycles to
optimize it, but the potential exists to really scream at runtime.  This
is the bloat involved in RISC architectures.

RAM and disk space are cheap nowadays.  CPU time is free when you
consider that FX!32 runs in the background or can be scheduled to run at
night when you're asleep (oops, or should be... :^)  CPU real estate is
still relatively expensive.

Goodnight,
Aaron
