Paul DeMone wrote:
> 
> Dennis Moran wrote:
> >
> > james.scannell <james.scannell@MCI2000.com> wrote in article
> > <01bc446d$ebf8b3c0$542437a6@scannell.mci.com>...
> > > Has Digital been able to improve the performance of FX!32 to the promised
> > > 70% of native Alpha speed after optimization? Last I heard it was still
> > > closer to 30%. At 70% Alpha systems would get more appealing.
> > >
> >
> > A 433MHz 21164 is about 10% faster than a Pentium Pro 200MHz running FX!32
> > binaries. The next version of FX!32 coming out this summer should up that
> > to around 100-150% faster.
> 
>     It would be nice if this was true but it doesn't compute.  If you
>     compare native code performance on SPECint95, a 433 MHz 21164A with
>     4 MB L3 runs 13.3 vs 8.2 for the P6/200/256KB, or only about 60% faster.
>     Am I missing something here?

I think so...I've seen numerous benchmarks (and performed a number myself)
that show for code heavy in floating point operations the 500MHz Alpha 
(1mb L3 cache) is about 3.0 - 4.0 times faster depending on the code.  We're
seeing a very consistent 3.5 time speedup for nearly all our codes when compared
to our 180 MHz PPro.  How this relates to spec numbers I have no idea.

I think spec numbers give you a general idea of processor performance,
but definitely don't tell the whole story.

Jeff



