This has been one of my major objections to going Alpha as well.  If they 
were open to more options, then great...

except for one other point.  No one has yet answered my question (only 
just posted it yesterday morning or so) concerning the type of memory 
that a DEC Alpha system requires.  I seem to recall from conversations 
with friends in my university's computer working group center that the 
DEC Alpha uses propriatory memory modules that is not only not compatible 
with non-Alpha systems, but is also not compatible with other Alpha 
versions!  

Hence, the cost of going Alpha has to include not only the loss of a 
reasonably large segment of your favorite software/games, but also 
kissing memory compatibility and lower cost goodbye.  You'd have to get 
memory from DEC and do it every time you went up a few notches in Alpha 
processor (along with a new board)...unless they have switched to more 
friendly and compatible memory usage (?)

At least with x86s and Apples, you can use the same memory on the older 
up to the latest processor generations (within some very minor limits).

patrick



