In article <334926C4.41C6@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu>, Jeffrey Potoff 
<jpotoff@latoso.cheme.cornell.edu> writes:
|>I'm running DEC Fortran 90 under Digital Unix and it's having
|>a hard time digesting numbers with large exponents.  If I do 
|>this:
|>	
|>	program crap
|>
|>	double precision test
|>
|>	test = 1e100
|>
|>	end
|>
|>When I compile it gives me:
|>
|>90: Error: crap.f, line 5: When converting this constant to a
|>REAL(KIND=4) number, underflow or overflow occurred.   [1E100]
|>        test = 1e100
|>
|>?????  Under IBM's xlf I can run exponents up the 300.  Is there
|>a way around this besides rewritting huge amounts of code.
|>I'm hoping that I'm just missing something simple.

Yep - and I'm somewhat astonished that the IBM compiler let you get away with
this.  1E100 is a *SINGLE PRECISION* constant, and is thus limited to a range
of about 1E38.  If you want double-precision, use 1D100.  This is the standard
way to do it.

Digital's F77 compilers would let this go, and give you a double-precision
value when assigned to a double-precision variable.  But Fortran 90 tightened
up the rules regarding this and Digital Fortran 90 follows suit.  You can
get the old behavior by compiling with -fpconstant.
-- 

