OK, OK, there were none in this case.  My statement was meant to be
directed at
the odd individuals who seem to have some sort of stake in recommending
an 
Alpha box for normal home users.  This is disegenuous because it leaves
out 
primary point I have tried to repeatedly make so no one is fully misled.

An Alpha computer is fast.  No doubt at all.  But they are STILL
specialty
boxes that would ill serve the standard home user due to software 
incompatibilities inherent in NT, the only possible os choice for a home
user in the case of the Alpha platform.

Actually, I find it rather sad (and frustrating) that yet another
hardware
company is falling for Microsloth.  It would be real nice it DEC threw 
its lot to the SUN, IBM, Motorola, Apple group for the pure Java
movement.
No more need for MS anything.  They could also really get on to the Open
everything wagon:  OpenGL, Open32, OpenDOC, etc.  Then it would be set
to be a future-great-choice for home use since mucho software written in
a platform- and os-independent manner could be run on it.

I would like to be able to really consider an Alpha but with NT, the 
software just isn't there and you screw up a really nice design with
a bloated, overpriced operating system which immediately knocks it out
of the running for normal home users.

THIS is what potential buyers need to know, NOT just the cheerleading
from
a handful of true believers who seem to believe that all that froo-froo
home software, that major investment by the home user, those games for 
the home user and their kids, are easily tossed and forgotten simply for
the prestige of owning an Alpha which is way overpowered for what they 
want to do anyway.  

