Subject: Help solve mystery: DEC floppy drive-induced melt-down!
Date: 1997/04/07
Message-ID: <5ibsrb$ok6$1@nntp.Stanford.EDU>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 231467687
Organization: Stanford University
Reply-To: dtrojan@mail.sas.upenn.edu
Newsgroups: comp.sys.laptops,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.misc,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.storage,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.systems


I recently had to replace a floppy drive on a DEC notebook (model
DECpc425SLC). After some confusion at the service center, I was sold a
drive by a different manufacturer (Canon orig., NEC replacement). No
problem, though, since they were both listed under the same part no.
(29-30630-01, in case any of you are _that_ in the know).

Here's the puzzle: I got home and popped it in (again, no apparent
problems), then turned it on. On came the BIOS info, etc. and right
when it would have gone to the drive. . . the screen started to invert
black and white from the outside in, something like tunnel vision
before you black out.

I turned off the machine within a couple of seconds, gave it a minute
and nervously tried again. Nothing at all this time. Power indications
light up on the case, but the screen and drive don't even turn over.
Dead by the side of the road.

So I call the service center: "Our Engineers say that it is impossible
for a floppy drive to damage a laptop." Although I would like to
believe them, my naive sense of the timing of the accident and
rudamentary understanding of circuits makes me wonder if there isn't
some way a flawed drive could have "blown" something else.

Without blaming the drive, even, could I have put the connecting coil
in in such a way as to cause a melt-down? (The layout is such that the
keyboard lifts up to reveal only half the battery, and the hard and
floppy drives--I really didn't have the chance to bump/break any other
parts of the system board.)

Finally, does the screen behavior suggest any diagnosis? As you might
guess, this machine is closer to the pasture than $200/hr. servicing,
so unless I can figure this one out myself. . .

Thanks for any help. (email is preferred, but post if more convenient)

dtrojan@mail.sas.upenn.edu




