Scott S wrote:
> 
> I'd get an Alpha in a heartbeat if their FX!32 emulation software was
> in the form of firmware.  The software creates too much overhead.  If
> I'm going to give up some of my apps I'd have to do it for something
> that isn't going to take a large hit in emulation.
> 

Hang on a second - what makes you think that FX!32 in software
creates any more overhead than dumping the code onto a chip?
As many people have pointed out, once you've run your x86 
program a few times, it's mostly been converted to Alpha code anyway, 
and requires practically no interference from FX!32 from that 
point on. Don't think of it as an emulator, think of it as a
compiler that (mostly) takes x86 machine code as source and
produces Alpha machine code. You wouldn't ask GNU to put GCC
in firmware to make the object files run faster...

I think the advantages of having FX!32 in software - more flexible
configuration, low cost (a few MB of RAM to load into), and
easy customer upgrades to take advantage of new features and
algorithm improvements - outweigh any marginal benefits of 
trying to get it into hardware.

