"Dennis Moran" <coolio@coolio9.com> writes:
> This is hardware emulation. Software emulation is totally different, and
> usually slow and unoptimized.
--

That's precisely my point - although it's not 'usual', it's nothing
very different than what Intel had to do with the PentiumPro, and
certainly nothing more than they'll have to do with Merced. For
certain code sequence the PPRO still uses sequenced microcode to
effect the same code. Sounds like software to me.

At ISCA'97 (Intl. Symp. on Computer Architecture) in June in Denver,
there will be an interesting paper by VLIW researchers at IBM showing
a design to accelerate the rapid translation of e.g., x86 (or PPC)
code into a native VLIW format. The technique used is to software
translate pages of "hosted" programs into "native" programs, and
provide hardware support for automatically re-vectoring program flow.
This particular system is envisioned to have a combined software and
hardware component.

In some sense, you could think of the Alpha as having made similar
architectural modifications to facilitate binary translation as a
reasonable alternative to hardware implementation of the Win32/IA32
instruction set. In particular, I'm thinking of the byte load/store
operations in the EV56, 21164PC and 21264. I'm certain these will help
improve performance somewhat.



-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



