Maybe not.  I know from hard experience that the VS2000 floppy driver
in NetBSD has a bug that prevents you from reading or writing RX50's
in a RX33 drive.  It may be the case that the RQDX3 driver has the
same bug.  If there isn't currently such a bug in the RQDX3 driver,
I'm willing to bet that next week it'll be introduced :-).  I've
never seen an operating system other than NetBSD where working device 
drivers were so efficiently broken in new releases.

>  Although, actually, I can think of a reason to bother: the RX50 looks cool.
>:)

Yeah, but it's mechanically a nightmare.  The TEAC mechanism used in
the RX33 is far more robust.  Just wait until the belt in the RX50
falls off and you have to figure out how to restring it such that
the motor - which has an axis perpendicular to the floppy spindles -
will turn the upper spindle clockwise and the lower one counterclockwise.

