On Sat, 5 Apr 1997, Dennis Moran wrote:

> > I would also like to know if this FX!32 means you can run x86 software 
> > only under NT and thus, _only_ software that is compatible with NT.
> Yes, only under NT. What does it matter?

Err?  It matters because there is a LOT of software that is not 
compatible with NT, thus, if you buy the Alpha with its FX!32 and NT, 
then you have essentially tossed out a decent segment of your software 
investment and much of your future desires.  This is particularly true 
with games, for which NT truly sucks. 

Now you cannot blow this off as insignificant because much of this talk 
goes to the use of an Alpha system for a typical home user.  Home users 
play games, games are the single biggest software moneymaker/salesmaker.  
A home user can therefore forget the Alpha for now until and unless NT 
game compatibility improves...or perhaps you would like to suggest that 
such home users cough up another few grand for a true X86 box with Win95 
on it so they can play their games, leaving the Alpha to do taxes and 
wordprocessing, which I am sure we can agree is a waste of the system.  
Home users do not do major scientific math-intensive programs.  They 
don't do extensive image processing.  They don't do major spreadsheet 
calculations.  These items would do well on an Alpha system, so an Alpha 
is a good special use box.  For normal home use, Windows 3.1 is STILL the 
largest home os (beaten only by DOS), followed by Win95 (NOT NT by a long 
shot - there are MANY more OS/2 users than there are NT users.  I also 
believe that there may be more Linux users than NT users as well but I 
don't as clearly recall that section of the survey).

The only people I can see pushing Alphas for home use are employees of 
the quickly shrinking DEC workforce or Microsloth hacks who are trying to 
push bloated MS oses.  

YOU might find the Alpha a useful tool at home but most other (read 
"typical") users would not.

patrick



