You get bonus points for proper use of the word 'usually'.

A System/370 emulator was written to run on an early predecessor to the
RS/6000.  In some important cases, it ran codes faster than the fastest
System/370 implementation.  Why?  Because the RISC box had many more
physical registers than did the S/370, and the emulator did a better
job of optimization than did the hardware.

No reason to suspect this can't/won't happen for the x86 emulators.

However I don't understand your use of 'unoptimized'.  It seems to me
that any serious emulator does optimization.  Which emulators do you
know of that are 'unoptimized'?

