> On Sun, 6 Apr 1997, Patrick wrote:
> > On Sat, 5 Apr 1997, Dennis Moran wrote:
> > A home user can therefore forget the Alpha for now until and unless NT 
> > game compatibility improves...or perhaps you would like to suggest that 
> No NT thanks... Still use DOS for games.
I would otherwise agree except...

id Software has indicated that Win95 is their future focus, not DOS.  
Interplay has indicated that Win95, not DOS is their future focus for 
games.  It continues with other developers.  Unfortunately, the game 
developers are tagging along with Microsloth Windoze, not DOS for future 
offerings.  Your DOS offerings are shrinking and will begin to soon drop 
steeply in availability.  I don't like it at all, would prefer an 
os-independent solution, but for now that isn't happening.

> > Home users do not do major scientific math-intensive programs.
> Sorry, you are wrong there. And it will increase in the next few
> years.

Please.  What fraction, do you suppose, of the home market will be doing 
major scientific math-intensive projects at home?  Hell, the bulk of the 
using (and non-using) public is practically scientifically illiterate and 
you propose that they will be doing sci-calculations at home instead of 
emailing friends, playing games, doing taxes?  

I am a scientist and I (nor anyone else in the any of the labs around 
ours) needs to use any scientific software that is particularly onerous 
to either old Apple IIex's, PowerPCs, or any of the Intel boxes scattered 
about our building.  The only scientific program I actually make 
scattered use of at home is an RNA folding program that has to do some 
thermodynamic-related math...and it isn't onerous.  An Alph would be 
overkill. 

> 
> > They don't do extensive image processing.
> 
> Wrong again.  I've run jobs at home that runs for CPUdays.
> 

Again, you mistake yourself with the broader public at large.  Even the 
majority of those in this group are not representative of the MUCH larger 
using public, most of whom don't know what USENET is nor what newsgroups 
are.  As ISPs gain more and more customers, this will change a bit but by 
and large, I would bet my left testicle that most users will rely 
exclusively on the web, sans newsgroups and other computer forums.  Those 
who do intensive scientific work are a minority.  Those who do intensive, 
professional and semi-professional graphics work are a minority.  The 
vast majority barely knows how their computer works in the first place, 
has never written a single line of code, doesn't know what a compiler 
is.  Need I go on?  

> > They don't do major spreadsheet calculations.
> 
> Large stats work, rather than spreadsheets.
> 
> > YOU might find the Alpha a useful tool at home but most other (read 
> > "typical") users would not.
> 
> You forget that `home use', as opposed to to those who work from home,
> ie `home office', is still a very bimodal group; on one hand are the
> games users and the like, and the other big group is the computer enthusiast.
> Very little in common there. But, both groups would enjoy the speed of
> the alphas, and if you have both under the one roof, you really do need a
> good multi-user system.

I don't dispute this but...home workers are still a large minority.  Even 
so, they do not need to be particularly computer savvy to do their 
business.  Enthusiasts are a different animal, a group that I would 
identify myself with but in my case, I do not have an endless cash supply 
so it is impossible and unrealistic for me (and probably many, many 
others like me) to consider buying multiple systems or to keep changing 
systems as they come available.  And if part of one's computer enjoyment 
comes from playing games, then with an Alpha you are SOL.  For the most 
part, you speak of a rich man's hobby.

patrick



