Richard,

1. By fiddling with jumpers on the processor daughtercard, you can
easily upgrade the XL 590 to 133MHz.  If you need to have exact jumper
settings for doing so, let me know.

2. You can also go for a Pentium OverDrive processor (expensive) and
run it up to 166MHz, which is hardly worth the extortive price that
Intel (and dealers) charges for the beast.  After all, it's only 25%
faster than a 133.

3. The second socket on the daughtercard is for a second processor.
At least that's what my Celebris XL 590 manuals tell me.  It's for
running an OS that uses more than one processor, like Windows NT.

4. Intel and/or 3rd parties may announce other upgrade chips for your
box in the future, including possibly an MMX-capable Pentium upgrade.
Right now there is no reasonably priced MMX upgrade. 

5. You may or may not be able to install an AMD K5 or Cyrix/IBM 6x86
in your system.  In favor of DEC is that they appear not to have
mucked up the BIOS with weird proprietary features.  If you can get
one of these CPUs to try, it might be worth your while.  They give
more bang for the buck than Intel.

6. You can always spend really serious bucks and replace the
motherboard and daughter card with new ones from DEC.  But the DEC's
price to do so can almost buy you an entire new system! ... Ben Myers

