References: <1997Apr3.145612@cornell-iowa.edu>
Organization: UltraNet Communications, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Kyle,

When you installed the SCSI CD-ROM, you may have created a SCSI bus
termination problem.  If there is more than one SCSI device on the
SCSI bus (in addition to the controller), the bus must be terminated
at both ends, and not in the middle.  With just a single device and
controller, SCSI bus termination is almost a non-issue.

As a quick and dirty solution, try reversing the SCSI connectors
between the hard disk and the CD-ROM.  If the hard disk has its
termination enabled and it is physically in the middle of the SCSI
cable, the system SCSI host adapter won't "see" the CD-ROM when it
polls the SCSI bus (SCSI cable) for devices.

FYI, SCSI termination consists of nothing more than some resistors at
the end of the SCSI bus.  The resistors absorb the electronic signals
propagating down the SCSI bus, and don't allow them to bounce back
down the bus in the other direction.

Finally, make sure that the SCSI host adapter in your system has its
SCSI termination enabled.

Honestly, SCSI IS fairly easy to do.  Been doing SCSI PC systems for 7
or 8 years now... Ben Myers

