The tf8x7 series of tape drives are famous for jamming. The way to
remove your tape, and hopefully it is still in tack is, first put key
switch in front of unit to the sign that has the "hand" on it. This
unlocks the unit from the case, which allows you to side the unit out.
#2- go around back and in to upper right hand corner is a small opening
that you have to a small screwdriver into and push up on, this releases
a lever, and push out at the same time. Now the unit is able to be slide
out. #3- pull from front, also look at the length of the power and data
cable in back, you may have to disconnect them. pull unit out about s
inches, enought room to gain access to the lever on the right hand side,
pull lever down and the loader ass. will swing to your left and expose
the tape drive. Look over the top and you should see if the tape drives
"leader" is off the spool in back of unit or is hung. Any ways, to
remove the tape cartage, by looking from the front, look at the r. hand
front side of unit and push back on the round silver/gold cylinder and
this will release the tape itself, then you can remove the tape cartage
and the tape will spool out on you, but you can pull it out of the unit
and then reset the the "leader" back on to the pawl, which is behind the
square top box, in the middle of the unit. If the "leader" is broken or
looks bad replace it with DEC part # 74-40273-02. you can call dec
direct or a local DEC computer broker and get this part. If you do not
feel confident in replacing this, you can call someone else than DEC to
fix you problem and for a LOT less, folks like DecisionOne, their the
largest outfit, or a local mom and pop computer repair place. With all
the layoff's at DEC, their are a lot of really good repair folks out in
the market place that can fix you problems..
Regards,
Tim



