In comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.chips Paul Flaherty <flaherty@pago-pago.pa.dec.com> wrote:
: It depends on the linearity of the amplification.  Linear Amplifiers (Class A)
: are only about 40% efficient, while nonlinear amplifiers (Class C) are closer
: to 70%.  Your typical 100W HF Linear (Class AB) generally requires about 200W
: of input power, and dissipates 100W as waste heat.  OTOH, duty cycle is 
: usually pretty low.

: Note that most digital stuff is pretty nonlinear.
Yes indeed, typical CPU's use CMOS transistors which are normally off and
only consume power while they are switching (which is why increasing the
clock increases the power consumption and heat production)  There is at
least one company (can't remember) that is building PowerPC chips using
bipolar transistors.  This type is always on (like a Class A amp) and
consume wads of power all the time.  The advantage is that the switching
speed is inherently much faster.  This company is producing 400+ MHz PPC
chips, quite an improvement over the Motorola ~200 MHz CMOS design.

Chris J.



