Dan Pop (Dan.Pop@cern.ch) wrote:
> Well, transmitters are usually supposed to radiate RF power, not to 
> dissipate heat.  The heat dissipation is unavoidable, but I doubt the
> bulk of the power goes into heat.

It depends on the linearity of the amplification.  Linear Amplifiers (Class A)
are only about 40% efficient, while nonlinear amplifiers (Class C) are closer
to 70%.  Your typical 100W HF Linear (Class AB) generally requires about 200W
of input power, and dissipates 100W as waste heat.  OTOH, duty cycle is usually
pretty low.

Note that most digital stuff is pretty nonlinear.

