In article <33401AE0.55A14187@black1.m.isar.de>,
Georg E. Schwarzwaelder <georg@black1.m.isar.de> wrote:
>Hi,
>
>I bought an applicationDEC 433 MP (ISA based) for very less money. I
>have planned to replace the board with a PCI board and a Pentium ...
>
>But with delivery I got 3 CPU/SCSI boards and 3 memory boards
>additionally and for now I'm not sure to throw away all this nice
>things.
>
>Also I added the boards, installed Linux and built a kernel with SMP
>enabled. But the memory was still 16 MB and no additioal cpu was
>recognized
>
>Questions: Does anyone know, how to enable memory and cpu?
>or
>can I use the additional cpu's and memory (without installing SCO, I
>prefer Linux) or should I replace it with a PCI solution (throwing away
>the boards)
>
The 433MP is a Corallary C Bus I design.  Unless Linux has a driver to
deal with such a system, the extra CPUs and memory above 16MB are
not available.  SCO UNIX is the only OS I know of which supports this.
While SCO does have a "Free UNIX" license for personal use, I doubt
the MPX (aka SMP) package is included.  Couldn't hurt to check their
web site.

Also, there used to be an EISA upgrade kit for around $1K US.  People
have also reported replaceing the 486/33 CPUs with /66s but it was
never tested/supported.  I doubt it would handle much more.  Even with
one CPU, the SMP logic slows things down.  It is only if the extra
processors can be used that it shines.

Nice system for the day - 1990.

Sam




