I am having a problem with a memory leak when I use one of the associative
containers such as map or set in the Standard Template Library. This
problem occures on a Digital Alpha running Digital Unix 3.2D-1 and DEC C++
5.4. Actually, it's more like a river than a leak! The problem occures
when I have a class which contains more than one instance of a particular
associative container.
Exmaple:

class foo
{
   map<int, int, less<int> > x1;
   map<int, int, less<int> > x2;
};

int main()
{
   foo *pMyfoo;
   while (1)
   {
      pMyfoo = new foo();
      delete pMyfoo;
   }
}

When I run this, even though I call delete on pMyfoo, the memory being
used is not getting completely released, eventually causing the program to
run out of heap. If I change the class foo to have only one instance of a
map, the problem goes away. Also, when I use other containers such as
vector or deque in place of map, I don't have any problems. I tried to
replicate this problem using Visual C++ 4.2 on an Intel machine, but it
worked fine. Does anybody have an idea what is going on?

