A beta-release of the ASCII->HTML conversion utility AscToHTM is now available 
as shareware.  See 

		http://www.yrl.co.uk/~jaf/asc2htmL.html

for details.  This software was developed under VMS using C++, though it is 
largely platform-independent.  A PC version will follow in a week or so.

The version available will work until the end of May 1997.  

The software analyses your text to determine the "document policy" for that
file, that is the layout rules used by the author.  It then uses these rules
when generating the HTML to reduce error rates.  The HTML generated will

	- convert URLs, email addresses, newsgroups etc to hyperlinks
	- insert anchors before each section
	- convert cross-references to hyperlinks
	- match the original indentaion levels
	- mark up bullet points
	- spot pre-formatted text and preserve it (in <PRE>...</PRE> markups)

Users can override any or all of the document policy to correct any mistakes 
in the analysis, though hopefully that won't be necessary.

The user's document policy can also be used to "add value" to the HTML, 
allowing the program to:

	- add title, colour and background images
	- split the document into smaller pages
	- generate a hyperlink contents list
	- add hyperlinks to key words through a "link dictionary"
	- add standard HTML headers, footers and Javascript to the generated
	  pages.
	- generate files with DOS-compatible names

Full on-line documentation is available at

	http://www.yrl.co.uk/~jaf/a2h.htm

or downloadable from

