strange@zk3.dec.com (Steve Strange USG) writes:

+In article <5gv4t0$4a3@engnews1.Eng.Sun.COM> robinson@jetsun.eng.sun.com (David Robinson) writes:

+   >But there are studies that show AFS (and I imagine Transarc DFS) gives
+   >10 times the network performance. Certainly worth a call if you're in
+   >the unix space and have a large set of systems using the files...
+   >it was meant to scale to 10s of thousands of systems, multiple
+   >organizations etc...not I suspect the nfs design center...

+   Be careful when reading those studies.  Most are very old and
+   are based on slow machines or concentrate on performance of
+   rarely used operations (create, mkdir).  AFS and DFS has advantages
+   but I doubt that you will find an overall 10X network performance
+   speedup.  Peak SPEC SFS (aka LADDIS) numbers show NFS servers doing
+   over 24,000 operations/sec over 12 100mbps Ethernets which is likely
+   to represent anywhere from 5000-15000 clients.

+LADDIS measures the efficiency/performance of an NFS server, but not
+the efficiency (or lack thereof) of the NFS protocol!  A major
+advantage of DFS is its more efficient protocol, which means less load
+on the network for the same overall client load.  Not to mention the
+cache coherency, security, and global namespace advantages that DFS
+has over NFS.  NFS is successful because it's relatively simple, and
+was easy to port to UNIXes (and Sun was willing, perhaps anxious, to
+license it initally).  And most people seem not to care about cache
+coherency (Like PC users who don't seem mind rebooting several times a
+day when their system hangs or crashes :-) ).  NFS's statelessness
+does make server crash recovery trivial (save for file locks), which
+is certainly an advantage, and admittedly a bit of a hole in AFS/DFS.
+Also I think NFS has gotten more widespread attention in terms of
+performance tuning and protocol improvements (V3) due to its
+popularity.

Of course, as usual it depends on what your are doing with it. Our 
department has shifted to AFS almost four years ago and at times I 
have found myself extremely frustrated with its performance. The
write throughput was more or less than of a floppy disk, the last
time I measured it (100Kb/sec). The overhead to contact the server
is simply too great and I haven't found a user configurable way 
to alleviate the problem.

In the AFS world, there are these users sitting on their machines that 
more or less execute the same things again and again. Now, my typical 
cycle is: compile on machine a, execute on machines c1 to c16. You can 
imagine what this does to the AFS cache algorithms. At least, I tend 
to get consistent results since the file is copied over to local disk 
avoiding network traffic while executing. 

As for large runs, I have to compile like 3 GB worth of executables 
(2-4MB each). I do my compiles on an sun server, with 12 processors 
and 4GB worth of memory. Unfortunately, it has this tiny 300 MB AFS 
cache. As you can imagine, whenever I start parallel compile, I can 
bring the serve to its knees. There is plenty of memory and idle cycles 
left but the AFS cache is getting thrashed until it throws everything 
out of there. It's also easy to imagine how the poor AFS server feels 
getting hit with all this traffic. Anyway, I do the compiles locally 
and only at the end, I copy the executable over AFS. Still, AFS 
throughput is what limits how fast I can finish the compilations. 
Recently, the sysadm increased the AFS memory cache parameters and 
things seem a bit better but nothing dramatic.

						Yannis



