In article <15039@raven.ukc.ac.uk>, weh1@ukc.ac.uk (W.E.Hargrove) writes:

:I wonder if some one can tell me what a "VAX Station 3100 VS42A-BA CPU
:KA42" is? Excuse my total ignorance, but does this refer to the physical
:chip, or the base unit box.

   The VAXstation 3100 is the marketing name for the system.  VS42A-BA
   denotes the system packaging.  KA42 denotes the processor module.
   The "model" designation was added later, with the early systems
   receiving 30 and 40.  (The latter has a slightly taller box and
   more storage bays, but is otherwise identical to the former.)
   (And based on my dim recollections, this looks like a model 30.)

:If anyone can point me in a direction where I can find out more about this
:machine I would very much appreciate it. Also, is it possible to connect a
:DEC VR 290-DA or VR 290-D3 monitor to it.

   It's an old (and slow) VAXstation.  Assuming that all modules normally
   found in this system are still present, it should be able to drive
   a VR290-class monitor.  (There were two graphics controllers available
   on this series, the older GPX in 1 or 8 planes, and the then-more-recent
   SPX 8 plane module.  Both support the VR290 class monitors.)

   These boxes often have a SCSI adapter.  If so, do not exceed 1.073 GB
   on the system disk capacity -- this is a limitation of all VAXstation
   3100 series systems.  These boxes also often have an Ethernet adapter.

   The ULTRIX and OpenVMS operating systems are supported on these boxes.

