In article <5he7a7$ofu@client2.news.psi.net>, genanr@thumper2.emsphone.com (Andrew N. Ryan) writes:
:	I would like to hear from anybody that has experience with clustering
:ALPHA systems....

   I will assume OpenVMS and a VMScluster here, as you do not explicitly
   mention an operating system.

   A VMScluster is more tightly coupled than a traditional network, but
   is less tightly coupled (and somewhat slower) than an SMP system.
   As an example, the OpenVMS scheduler operates across an SMP system,
   but not across a VMScluster.  And the distributed lock manager
   operates across a uniprocessor, and SMP system, and across members
   of a VMScluster, but not across any nodes not participating in the
   VMScluster.

   An assortment of VMScluster interconnects are available, ranging from
   SCSI or Ethernet on the low-end to memory channel and CI on the high
   end.  Alpha systems are quite capable of running an 802.3/Ethernet at
   the specified limit all day long -- if inter-node or intra-cluster
   performance is a key consideration, look at FDDI or fast Ethernet.

   For information on VMSclusters, see:
     http://www.openvms.digital.com/openvms/products/clusters/index.html

	--

   The quorum scheme means a two-node VMScluster is usually configured
   with a quorum disk on a shared interconnect, to allow it to survive
   the loss of any signle node.  Three (or more) nodes in a VMScluster
   means one can often dispense with the quorum disk, and still survive
   the failure of any single node.  (You'll want to look at the OpenVMS
   manual set for information on properly configuring a VMScluster...)

