In comp.sys.dec Jeff Shirley <slinker@primenet.com_> wrote:
> I have what I believe to be an older KA650-BA CPU.  The revision number stamped
> on the card is C1.  It describes itself on system restart as:
>
> KA650-B V1.2/0123

  KA650's below rev K (or thereabouts) have defective Q-bus interface chips
(QBIC) which cause them to not work with many non-DEC NPR devices (and some
DEC ones like the TSV05 controller). I've never seen the "good" command set
on pre-rev-K boards, so there may be some prerequsite.
  
> Can this be "upgraded" to a KA650-AA via firmware swap, or are there other
> differences between the -BA and the -AA?

  Yes. Part of the SID (System ID register) is writable, and the EPROM code
contains the data for it. The only difference between the 2 is the license
bits in the SID.

> DEC87 LM8840 070E7
> DEC87 LM8840 071E7

  That's 23-070E7-00 (or perhaps 23-070E7-01). If you call DECdirect at
800-DIGITAL between 9:00 AM and 5:00 PM EST and ask the Happy People that
answer for "part number assistance", you'll be connected to someone who
can answer the question "Part 23-070E7-00 [-01] has been replaced. Can
you tell me the new part number?".

  That will get you the part numbers for the current version of those
chips.

> I am not trying to sidestep any licensing issues here; my interest is in
> expanding the console command set.  I would really like to be able to do a
> SHOW QBUS or SHOW BOOT, but these commands are not implemented on my board.

  If you change from a -BA to an -AA (or the other way) the existing licenses
won't load because they're a different processor type code.

