jfkirk@grove.ufl.edu wrote:
: Sean Leung wrote:
: > I was given an OEM version of a DEC LSI - 11/23 computer.
: > 
: > It is an ADAC System 1000, with a Q-bus backplane and power supply.
: > 
: > These are the boards inside -
: > 
: > M8186 (KDF11AC cpu card)
: > 
: > M8044 DH and M8044 DC memory boards
: > 
: > M8047 multifunction card (serial i/o and prom sockets - no proms
: > present)
: > 
: > M8013 and M8014 RL01 hard disk controllers
: > 
: > M8012 terminator card (with some proms on it)
: > 
: > one, RL02 disk drive and hard disk pack (with RT-11 and other programs
: > on it)
: > 
: > I can get the machine to power-up and come to the @ prompt, but I can't
: > get it to boot-up RT-11 O.S. or read from the drive.
: > 
: > Schuland Leung
: Type 173000g at the @ prompt.  This should boot the computer.

	WATCH OUT !
	I recall that many of these ADAC 1000 systems which predate Q22
put +-!12V on the Qbus to supply their ADAC cards. These non-standard power
supply lines are placed on the same backplane lines as the Q22 extended
address lines. So if you insert a Q22-capable Qbus card into such a chassis
you will zap that card.
	As I recall, the first couple of slots were "exempt" from this, so
the CPU (11/23 which is Q22-capable) is not in danger.
	It is possible to remove the +-12V (or was it 15V) supply from these
backplanes quite easily. However I think the backplane still does not support
Q22. It could probably be hand-wired though.

