In article <5hrif6$ii@nnrp1.news.primenet.com>, Jeff Shirley <slinker@primenet.com_> writes:
|>Greetings.
|>
|>I have what I believe to be an older KA650-BA CPU.  The revision number stamped
|>on the card is C1.  It describes itself on system restart as:
|>
|>KA650-B V1.2/0123
|>

Bletch! That looks really, really old to me. The latest version of which I
am aware is 
	KA650-A V5.3, VMB 2.7

(this in response to a SHOW VER command - but you get much the same 
on a power-cycle).

|>Can this be "upgraded" to a KA650-AA via firmware swap, or are there other
|>differences between the -BA and the -AA?
|>
|>The two 27512 EPROMs on my CPU are labeled:
|>
|>DEC87 LM8840 070E7
|>DEC87 LM8840 071E7
|>
|>I am not trying to sidestep any licensing issues here; my interest is in
|>expanding the console command set.  I would really like to be able to do a
|>SHOW QBUS or SHOW BOOT, but these commands are not implemented on my board.
|>

The -AA variant is multiuser (i.e. sold as a MicroVAX), the -BA variant is
single user (i.e. sold as a VAXserver). Going from -BA to -AA required that
you pay.

Your real problem is that you want the latest version of the -BA firmware.
These days it's not going to be very easy to do that cheaply (assuming the
parts can be readily found). My guess is that the cheapest legal alternative
would be to purchase a KA650 (or better yet a KA655) from the second-hand
system vendor of your choice. (Note that the KA655 F/W is housed in a single
EPROM rather than in two EPROMs as in the KA650).


