Dave Mitton wrote:
> 
> Mark Atwood <zot@ampersand.com> wrote:
> 
> >
> >Just what is DECnet?
> 
> A marketing term for the entire suite of networking components developed by
> Digital.  Each major set of features releases were dubbed a "Phase".

[snip]
 
> DECnet also includes a number of helper and applications protocols such as:
> MOP - for boot and maintenance operations
> NCP - Network control Protocol

Ohps!

> DAP - Data Access Protocol for file xfer and record access
> CTERM - Terminal operation protocol
> LAT - Local Area Terminal protocol (while technically not of the same design
> roots... it is a part of the umbrella)
> 

NCP stands for 'Network Control Program', the tool to manage a DECnet
node. The protocol used by NCP and NML (Network Management Listener, the
component used when managing a node from another node) was NICE until
DECnet Phase IV.

In DECnet Phase V, the management tool is called NCL, the remote
component CML and the protocol used beetween them is CMIP.

